2026-07-04 15:21:44 [claude-sonnet-5] The standard, portable signatures for `main` in C++ are:
2026-07-04 15:21:44 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-07-04 15:21:44 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-07-04 15:21:44 [claude-sonnet-5] int main();
2026-07-04 15:21:44 [claude-sonnet-5] int main(int argc, char *argv[]);
2026-07-04 15:21:44 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-07-04 15:21:44 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-07-04 15:21:44 [claude-sonnet-5] or equivalently with pointer-to-pointer syntax:
2026-07-04 15:21:44 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-07-04 15:21:44 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-07-04 15:21:44 [claude-sonnet-5] int main(int argc, char **argv);
2026-07-04 15:21:44 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-07-04 15:21:44 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-07-04 15:21:44 [claude-sonnet-5] `argv[argc]` is guaranteed to be a null pointer, and `main` must return `int` (returning nothing is
2026-07-04 15:21:44 [claude-sonnet-5] treated as `return 0;` in C++, unlike C which requires an explicit return in older standards).
2026-07-04 15:21:44 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-07-04 15:21:44 [claude-sonnet-5] On macOS specifically, there's also a non-standard third parameter some platforms support:
2026-07-04 15:21:44 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-07-04 15:21:44 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-07-04 15:21:44 [claude-sonnet-5] int main(int argc, char *argv[], char *envp[]);
2026-07-04 15:21:44 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-07-04 15:21:44 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-07-04 15:21:44 [claude-sonnet-5] That third `envp` parameter (the environment variable array) is supported on macOS (it's inherited
2026-07-04 15:21:44 [claude-sonnet-5] from BSD/POSIX conventions) but isn't part of the C++ standard, so it's best avoided unless you
2026-07-04 15:21:44 [claude-sonnet-5] specifically need direct access to the environment array without calling `getenv`/`environ`.
2026-07-04 15:21:44 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-07-04 15:21:44 [claude-sonnet-5] For your `macossnapshot.cpp`, you'd want:
2026-07-04 15:21:44 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-07-04 15:21:44 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-07-04 15:21:44 [claude-sonnet-5] int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
2026-07-04 15:21:44 [claude-sonnet-5] 	return 0;
2026-07-04 15:21:44 [claude-sonnet-5] }
2026-07-04 15:21:44 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-07-04 15:21:44 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-07-04 15:21:44 [claude-sonnet-5] That matches the pattern already used in `win32snapshot.cpp`. Want me to flesh out the actual
2026-07-04 15:21:44 [claude-sonnet-5] snapshot capture logic for macOS using `CGDisplayCreateImage` (whole screen) and
2026-07-04 15:21:44 [claude-sonnet-5] `CGWindowListCreateImage` (foreground window), mirroring the BMP/JPG/PNG writer calls used in the
2026-07-04 15:21:44 [claude-sonnet-5] Windows version?
2026-07-04 15:21:44 [claude-sonnet-5] 
