2026-07-08 20:25:57 [grok-build-0.1] Here are the main alternatives to `charAt()`:
2026-07-08 20:25:57 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-07-08 20:25:57 [grok-build-0.1] Basic alternatives
2026-07-08 20:25:57 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-07-08 20:25:57 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-07-08 20:25:57 [grok-build-0.1] const friend = friends[0];           // Most common and idiomatic
2026-07-08 20:25:57 [grok-build-0.1] const friend = friends.at(0);        // Modern (supports negative indices)
2026-07-08 20:25:57 [grok-build-0.1] const friend = friends.slice(0, 1);
2026-07-08 20:25:57 [grok-build-0.1] const friend = friends.substring(0, 1);
2026-07-08 20:25:57 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-07-08 20:25:57 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-07-08 20:25:57 [grok-build-0.1] Better options for your emoji string
2026-07-08 20:25:57 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-07-08 20:25:57 [grok-build-0.1] Your string contains emojis (which are made of surrogate pairs). `charAt(0)`, `[0]`, and `at(0)`
2026-07-08 20:25:57 [grok-build-0.1] will return broken characters (just the first half of the emoji).
2026-07-08 20:25:57 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-07-08 20:25:57 [grok-build-0.1] Better approaches:
2026-07-08 20:25:57 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-07-08 20:25:57 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-07-08 20:25:57 [grok-build-0.1] // Using destructuring (cleanest)
2026-07-08 20:25:57 [grok-build-0.1] const [friend] = friends;
2026-07-08 20:25:57 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-07-08 20:25:57 [grok-build-0.1] // Using spread
2026-07-08 20:25:57 [grok-build-0.1] const friend = [...friends][0];
2026-07-08 20:25:57 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-07-08 20:25:57 [grok-build-0.1] // Using Array.from
2026-07-08 20:25:57 [grok-build-0.1] const friend = Array.from(friends)[0];
2026-07-08 20:25:57 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-07-08 20:25:57 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-07-08 20:25:57 [grok-build-0.1] Recommendation
2026-07-08 20:25:57 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-07-08 20:25:57 [grok-build-0.1] For your specific case, I'd go with one of these:
2026-07-08 20:25:57 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-07-08 20:25:57 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-07-08 20:25:57 [grok-build-0.1] const [friend] = friends;           // clean and readable
2026-07-08 20:25:57 [grok-build-0.1] // or
2026-07-08 20:25:57 [grok-build-0.1] const friend = [...friends][0];
2026-07-08 20:25:57 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-07-08 20:25:57 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-07-08 20:25:57 [grok-build-0.1] These properly handle the emojis in your string.
