2026-07-08 20:43:42 [grok-build-0.1] 🟠nitrologic Relay 1.9.5 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪠 0.7° $0.007 21.57KB 12.04s
2026-07-08 20:43:42 [grok-build-0.1] No, you cannot nullify or strip the `export` modifier from the outside when loading a file directly
2026-07-08 20:43:42 [grok-build-0.1] into HTML.
2026-07-08 20:43:42 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-07-08 20:43:42 [grok-build-0.1] The presence of `export` makes the file a JavaScript module. Regular `<script src="...">` tags do
2026-07-08 20:43:42 [grok-build-0.1] not support module syntax, so the browser will throw a syntax error if it sees `export`.
2026-07-08 20:43:42 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-07-08 20:43:42 [grok-build-0.1] Your realistic options are:
2026-07-08 20:43:42 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-07-08 20:43:42 [grok-build-0.1] - Load the file using `<script type="module">` and then use proper `import` statements (this is the
2026-07-08 20:43:42 [grok-build-0.1] correct modern way).
2026-07-08 20:43:42 [grok-build-0.1] - Remove or comment out the `export` keyword in the source file itself so it becomes a regular
2026-07-08 20:43:42 [grok-build-0.1] script.
2026-07-08 20:43:42 [grok-build-0.1] - Have the module itself expose the class to the global scope (the file would need to do this
2026-07-08 20:43:42 [grok-build-0.1] internally).
2026-07-08 20:43:42 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-07-08 20:43:42 [grok-build-0.1] There is no way to externally "nullify" the `export` at load time without preprocessing the file or
2026-07-08 20:43:42 [grok-build-0.1] using a bundler.
2026-07-08 20:43:42 [grok-build-0.1] 
