2026-07-09 18:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] Issues Found
2026-07-09 18:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-07-09 18:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] • 1. Duplicate case (`0xE4`)*
2026-07-09 18:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] You have `CPX Zero Page` implemented twice. The second one at the bottom will never be reached.
2026-07-09 18:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-07-09 18:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] • 2. Redundant / conflicting Carry handling*
2026-07-09 18:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] In several places (especially `ASL A`), you're calling `setCarry()` and then immediately doing
2026-07-09 18:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] direct bit manipulation again:
2026-07-09 18:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-07-09 18:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-07-09 18:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] let carry = (val >> 7) & 1;
2026-07-09 18:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] this.setCarry(carry);
2026-07-09 18:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] // ...
2026-07-09 18:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] if (carry) this.registers[register.ST] |= 0x01;
2026-07-09 18:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] else this.registers[register.ST] &= ~0x01;
2026-07-09 18:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-07-09 18:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-07-09 18:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] This is fragile. `setCarry()` should be the single source of truth.
2026-07-09 18:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-07-09 18:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] • 3. `setStatus()` behavior*
2026-07-09 18:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] Your `setStatus()` only touches Z and N. This is mostly fine, but you're calling it in places where
2026-07-09 18:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] you shouldn't overwrite other flags (like after ADC/SBC where you already set V and C manually).
2026-07-09 18:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] Currently it works because you set C/V before calling `setStatus()`, but it's easy to
2026-07-09 18:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] break.
2026-07-09 18:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-07-09 18:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] • 4. JSR stack write is slightly off*
2026-07-09 18:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-07-09 18:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] this.memory[0x0100 + sp] = high;
2026-07-09 18:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] this.registers[register.SP]--;
2026-07-09 18:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] this.memory[0x0100 + sp - 1] = low;  // using old sp value
2026-07-09 18:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-07-09 18:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-07-09 18:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] This works, but it's confusing. Cleaner version:
2026-07-09 18:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-07-09 18:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] let sp = this.registers[register.SP];
2026-07-09 18:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] sp = (sp - 1) & 0xff;
2026-07-09 18:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] this.memory[0x0100 + sp] = (returnAddr >> 8) & 0xFF;
2026-07-09 18:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] sp = (sp - 1) & 0xff;
2026-07-09 18:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] this.memory[0x0100 + sp] = returnAddr & 0xFF;
2026-07-09 18:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] this.registers[register.SP] = sp;
2026-07-09 18:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-07-09 18:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-07-09 18:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] • 5. Missing `B` flag handling on interrupts*
2026-07-09 18:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] When you trigger `interrupt()`, you should be pushing the status with `B=0` (hardware interrupt).
2026-07-09 18:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] You're not pushing status at all in the current IRQ path.
2026-07-09 18:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-07-09 18:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] • 6. `read()` side effects*
2026-07-09 18:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] You're logging on every `0xc0xx` and `POKEY_RANDOM` read. This will get very noisy once the game is
2026-07-09 18:29:36 [grok-build-0.1] running properly.
