2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] I reviewed `machine.ts`. It is a compact, readable 6502/DVG work-in-progress, with useful trace
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] output and a sensible separation between CPU, vector generator, input, and display helpers.
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] A few issues stand out:
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] - TypeScript consistency
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] 	- `Machine` assigns `cyclecount` and `hasDVG`, but neither is declared as a class field.
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] 	- `frameCount` and `wordSize` lack explicit types.
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] 	- `LABS(address)` has an implicit `any` parameter.
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] 	- `dump(grid:[[]])` is too narrow for the `string[][]` passed by `buildMemcode`.
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] 	- `stepping` is inferred as `boolean`, but assignments such as `stepping=1-stepping` produce a
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] number.
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] 	- `reset()` requires four arguments, but the `R` key handler calls it with three.
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] - RTI status restoration is incorrect
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] 	- The RTI path uses `st=(st & ~0x14) | 0x20`.
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] 	- `0x14` clears both the Break and Interrupt Disable bits. RTI should restore the saved Interrupt
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] Disable state; only Break should be treated as non-persistent. This can cause interrupts to become
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] enabled unexpectedly after every RTI.
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] - Memory-mapped writes are bypassed in several instructions
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] 	- Some writes go through `writeAddress()`, but several write directly to `this.memory[address]`,
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] including:
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] 		- `STA (Indirect),Y`
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] 		- `LSR Absolute`
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] 		- `INC Absolute`
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] 		- `DEC Absolute`
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] 	- This will become significant for I/O registers, including DVG-related behaviour, because those
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] writes will not receive central handling.
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] - Timing is presently instruction-count based
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] 	- `tick()` always adds four cycles, although 6502 instruction timings vary considerably and
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] branches/page crossings have special timing.
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] 	- This is understandable while bringing the emulator up, but VBLANK, frame pacing, and game
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] behaviour will eventually need instruction-specific cycle counts.
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] - Interrupt behaviour
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] 	- `interrupt()` logs “interupt blocked by flag I” every time a VBLANK occurs while interrupts are
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] disabled. That will be very noisy during normal execution.
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] 	- The VBLANK latch logic is reasonable as a first pass, although its timing will depend on the
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] fixed-cycle issue above.
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] - DVG implementation is intentionally preliminary
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] 	- `LABS()` currently only uses two bytes for the beam position and logs an uncertainty note in the
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] surrounding code.
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] 	- `VEC()` and `SVEC()` decode/log words but do not yet render or update beam coordinates.
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] 	- The DVG call stack has no underflow/overflow checks, so malformed display lists could create
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] difficult-to-diagnose failures.
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] - Small cleanup opportunities
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] 	- `block` in `writeAddress()` is unused.
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] 	- `missingCodes` is never cleared, so repeated CRT refreshes may report the same codes
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] indefinitely.
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] 	- `readAddress()` stops the whole machine for any unimplemented POKEY address, which is useful for
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] diagnostics but may make incremental Atari support harder.
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] 	- There are a number of comments that still describe uncertainty or earlier experiments;
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] consolidating those once the hardware rules are confirmed would make the opcode implementation
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] easier to audit.
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] The main correctness priorities appear to be: fixing RTI’s status restoration, routing all writes
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] through `writeAddress()`, resolving the TypeScript declaration/call mismatches, and adding
2026-07-11 18:45:42 [gpt-5.6-terra] per-opcode cycle accounting.
