2026-07-13 21:28:45 [gpt-5.6-terra] A few notable issues stand out:
2026-07-13 21:28:45 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-13 21:28:45 [gpt-5.6-terra] - `reset` arguments are mismatched. Its signature is `(address, nmi, reset, irq, vectors)`,
2026-07-13 21:28:45 [gpt-5.6-terra] but initial startup calls:
2026-07-13 21:28:45 [gpt-5.6-terra]   - `game.reset(entry, nmi, irq, reset, true)`
2026-07-13 21:28:45 [gpt-5.6-terra]   - This swaps the IRQ and reset vectors.
2026-07-13 21:28:45 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-13 21:28:45 [gpt-5.6-terra] - The `R` key reset call is stale and invalid. It supplies four arguments where five are
2026-07-13 21:28:45 [gpt-5.6-terra] required, and passes `game.dvg.enabled` in the IRQ position. This should currently fail TypeScript
2026-07-13 21:28:45 [gpt-5.6-terra] checking.
2026-07-13 21:28:45 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-13 21:28:45 [gpt-5.6-terra] - `stepping` is declared as a boolean but assigned numbers through `stepping=1-stepping`.
2026-07-13 21:28:45 [gpt-5.6-terra] That is a TypeScript type error and should be boolean toggling instead.
2026-07-13 21:28:45 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-13 21:28:45 [gpt-5.6-terra] - `dump` has the wrong element type. It accepts `Array<Array<number>>`, while
2026-07-13 21:28:45 [gpt-5.6-terra] `buildMemcode` produces `string[][]`; both current calls to `dump(grid)` are incompatible under
2026-07-13 21:28:45 [gpt-5.6-terra] type checking.
2026-07-13 21:28:45 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-13 21:28:45 [gpt-5.6-terra] - `JSR` stack storage is off by one. With an initial `SP` of `FF`, the low byte is written
2026-07-13 21:28:45 [gpt-5.6-terra] to `01FD` rather than `01FE`. `RTS` then reads its low byte from `$01FE`, so returns will generally
2026-07-13 21:28:45 [gpt-5.6-terra] be corrupted.
2026-07-13 21:28:45 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-13 21:28:45 [gpt-5.6-terra] - `RTI` incorrectly clears the interrupt-disable flag.
2026-07-13 21:28:45 [gpt-5.6-terra]   `st=(st & ~0x14) | 0x20` clears both B (`10`) and I (`04`). RTI should restore I from the
2026-07-13 21:28:45 [gpt-5.6-terra] pulled status; only the synthetic break/unused-bit handling should be adjusted.
2026-07-13 21:28:45 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-13 21:28:45 [gpt-5.6-terra] - The reset/interrupt-vector naming is confusing enough to invite the first bug. `pc[2]`
2026-07-13 21:28:45 [gpt-5.6-terra] receives `irq`, `pc[3]` receives `reset`, while the caller uses variables in the opposite order.
2026-07-13 21:28:45 [gpt-5.6-terra] Making the API accept a named vector object would make this much safer.
2026-07-13 21:28:45 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-13 21:28:45 [gpt-5.6-terra] - Several writes bypass `writeAddress`. For example, `STA (Indirect),Y`, `INC Absolute`,
2026-07-13 21:28:45 [gpt-5.6-terra] `DEC Absolute`, and `LSR Absolute` write `this.memory[...]` directly. That will become inconsistent
2026-07-13 21:28:45 [gpt-5.6-terra] if memory-mapped write behaviour is expanded.
2026-07-13 21:28:45 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-13 21:28:45 [gpt-5.6-terra] The emulator structure is otherwise quite approachable: opcode handlers are explicit, the memory
2026-07-13 21:28:45 [gpt-5.6-terra] access abstraction is already in place, and the vector engine is cleanly separated from the 6502
2026-07-13 21:28:45 [gpt-5.6-terra] core.
