2026-07-14 19:38:15 [gpt-5.6-terra] Hi-Res Mixed Mode
2026-07-14 19:38:15 [gpt-5.6-terra] - Graphics area: 280 × 160 pixels
2026-07-14 19:38:15 [gpt-5.6-terra] - Text area: Bottom 4 text lines, or 32 pixels high
2026-07-14 19:38:15 [gpt-5.6-terra] - Commonly used for games and applications requiring status text.
2026-07-14 19:38:15 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-14 19:38:15 [gpt-5.6-terra] Video Memory
2026-07-14 19:38:15 [gpt-5.6-terra] - Text page size: 1 KB per page
2026-07-14 19:38:15 [gpt-5.6-terra] - Hi-res graphics page size: 8 KB per page
2026-07-14 19:38:15 [gpt-5.6-terra] - Standard Apple II supports:
2026-07-14 19:38:15 [gpt-5.6-terra] 	- Two text/lo-res display pages
2026-07-14 19:38:15 [gpt-5.6-terra] 	- Two hi-res display pages, when sufficient RAM is installed
2026-07-14 19:38:15 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-14 19:38:15 [gpt-5.6-terra] Notes on Variants
2026-07-14 19:38:15 [gpt-5.6-terra] - The original Apple II and Apple II Plus use the standard 40-column and 280 × 192
2026-07-14 19:38:15 [gpt-5.6-terra] graphics modes described above.
2026-07-14 19:38:15 [gpt-5.6-terra] - The Apple IIe added features such as lowercase support and optional 80-column text.
2026-07-14 19:38:15 [gpt-5.6-terra] - The Apple IIc and Apple IIGS provide further display capabilities, with the IIGS
2026-07-14 19:38:15 [gpt-5.6-terra] supporting substantially higher-resolution color graphics.
2026-07-14 19:38:15 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-14 19:41:09 [simon@midnightblue.local] memory locations of banks?
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] 🟠nitrologic Relay 1.9.5 gpt-5.6-terra 🌀 🪠 🧊 $0.017 2.923KB 11.70s
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] For the standard Apple II display pages, the important memory ranges are:
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] ╭──────────────────────────┬─────────────────┬─────────────────╮
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] │  Display area            │  Page 1         │  Page 2         │
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] ├──────────────────────────┼─────────────────┼─────────────────┤
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] │ ---                      │ ---:            │ ---:            │
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] │  Text / Lo-Res graphics  │  `$0400–$07FF`  │  `$0800–$0BFF`  │
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] │  Hi-Res graphics         │  `$2000–$3FFF`  │  `$4000–$5FFF`  │
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] ╰──────────────────────────┴─────────────────┴─────────────────╯
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] Text and Lo-Res
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] Lo-res graphics share the same memory as text mode.
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] - Text / Lo-Res Page 1: `0400–07FF`
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] - Text / Lo-Res Page 2: `0800–0BFF`
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] Each page is 1 KB, although not every byte necessarily corresponds to a visibly displayed character
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] or lo-res cell in the simplest linear sense.
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] Hi-Res Graphics
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] - Hi-Res Page 1: `2000–3FFF`
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] - Hi-Res Page 2: `4000–5FFF`
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] Each hi-res page occupies 8 KB. The visible 280 × 192 image uses a non-linear/interleaved address
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] layout within that range.
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] Apple IIe / Auxiliary Memory Banks
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] On an Apple IIe with 128 KB RAM, there is a main and an auxiliary 64 KB bank. The
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] same address ranges can exist in either bank:
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] ╭───────────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] │  Mode                             │  Main bank address                                │  Auxiliary bank address                          │
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] ├───────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] │ ---                               │ ---:                                              │ ---:                                             │
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] │  40-column text, normal graphics  │  Usually main `$0400–$0BFF`, `$2000–$5FFF`        │  Available but normally not displayed            │
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] │  80-column text                   │  Main + auxiliary `$0400–$07FF` or `$0800–$0BFF`  │  Each bank supplies alternating columns          │
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] │  Double Lo-Res                    │  Main + auxiliary text-page memory                │  Main/aux combine for greater horizontal detail  │
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] │  Double Hi-Res                    │  Main + auxiliary `$2000–$3FFF` or `$4000–$5FFF`  │  Main/aux combine for 560 × 192 output           │
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] ╰───────────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] For 80-column text and double hi-res, the display circuitry uses both main and auxiliary RAM at the
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] same addresses.
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] Language Card / Upper Memory Banks
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] On systems with a Language Card or equivalent Apple IIe banked RAM arrangement:
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] - `D000–DFFF` can refer to either of two switchable 4 KB RAM banks.
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] - `E000–FFFF` is the common 8 KB portion of Language Card RAM.
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] - Normally, this region contains ROM; soft switches select RAM visibility and write enable.
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] So the usual Language Card organization is:
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] ╭─────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] │  Address range  │  Bank arrangement                                     │
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] ├─────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] │  `$D000–$DFFF`  │  Bank 1 or Bank 2, selected by soft switch            │
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] │  `$E000–$FFFF`  │  Shared/common Language Card RAM                      │
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] │  `$D000–$FFFF`  │  ROM visible unless Language Card RAM is switched in  │
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] ╰─────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] The display page selection itself is controlled by video soft switches, not by changing these
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] memory addresses.
2026-07-14 19:41:21 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra] 🟠nitrologic Relay 1.9.5 gpt-5.6-terra 🌀 🪠 🧊 $0.067 68.71KB 14.94s
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra] A few notable findings from the shared code and trace:
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra] - The Apple II trace reaches `JMP (0036)` with `0036/0037 == 0000`, then BRKs.
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra]   That is why `COUT` lands at `$0000`. The Apple ROM expects monitor vectors such as `COUT` to have
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra] been initialised, or a minimal Apple II firmware/I/O environment to provide them. This is separate
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra] from CPU instruction emulation.
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra] - Lunar Lander’s NMI cadence is inconsistent with the frame timing and is likely causing the
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra] NMI-handler spin.
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra]   `tick()` is called per interpreted instruction and currently does:
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra]   - `cycleCount += 4` for every opcode, regardless of actual instruction duration.
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra]   - NMI every `333 * 12 = 3996` nominal cycles.
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra]   - frame duration at `16667` nominal cycles.
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra]   Thus NMIs occur about 4.17 times per displayed frame, and because an NMI is not masked by `I`,
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra] another NMI can interrupt an unfinished NMI handler. That can effectively prevent the handler from
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra] reaching `RTI`.
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra]   For a 1.5 MHz Atari 6502-family board at 60 Hz, a frame is roughly 25,000 CPU cycles; the
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra] interrupt schedule should derive from the selected machine clock and its video timing, rather than
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra] the current mixed cycle/microsecond constants.
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra] - The instruction cycle model needs to precede timing-sensitive emulation.
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra]   A fixed four cycles per instruction will skew interrupts, watchdogs, POKEY timing, and
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra] vector-generator polling. At minimum, each opcode should contribute its documented base cycle
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra] count, with branch-taken/page-cross penalties where applicable.
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra] - `R` reset command supplies the wrong arguments.
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra]   `Machine.reset` expects:
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra]   `reset(address, nmi, reset, irq, vectors)`
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra]   but the handler calls it with four arguments:
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra]   `game.reset(0x2000, 0x38c0, 0x3b4b, game.dvg.enabled);`
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra]   So `game.dvg.enabled` becomes the IRQ address and `vectors` is `undefined`. The regular startup
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra] call is correct.
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra] - Interrupt status-stack behaviour is slightly inaccurate.
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra]   For IRQ/NMI, the pushed status should have the break bit clear and the unused bit set. BRK should
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra] push break set. The current generic `interrupt()` pushes `st` unchanged. This may not explain the
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra] current spin, but software can inspect the stacked flags.
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra] - Several instructions bypass `writeAddress()` or `readAddress()`.
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra]   For example, `STA ($nn),Y`, some `INC`/`DEC`/shift absolute forms, and numerous zero-page reads
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra] use direct `memory[...]`. That works for RAM but bypasses I/O side effects. Once the Apple II soft
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra] switches or Atari device registers are modelled, those paths will need to go through the bus access
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra] functions.
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra] - The Battlezone watchdog spin at `$7B71` is expected ROM behaviour.
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra]   The code in `notes.txt` explicitly loops until a watchdog reset after detecting stack
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra] overflow/underflow. If execution arrives there, it is useful evidence of an earlier stack or
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra] interrupt-return issue, rather than the root cause itself.
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra] For Lunar Lander, I would first make NMI generation frame-based and ensure only one NMI is raised
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra] per intended vblank. Then verify that the handler returns via `RTI` before investigating ROM logic.
2026-07-15 11:09:26 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:10:31 [simon@midnightblue.local] explain apple2 init for COUT vector
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] 🟠nitrologic Relay 1.9.5 gpt-5.6-terra 🌀 🪠 🧊 $0.075 72.06KB 15.79s
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] `COUT` is an indirect output entry point in the Apple II monitor ROM.
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] In the trace:
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] FD8E  CROUT  LDA #$8D
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] FD90         BNE FDED
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] FDED  COUT   JMP ($0036)
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] `$FDED` does not itself write a character. It jumps through the 16-bit zero-page vector at:
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] $0036  CSWL   low byte of character-output routine
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] $0037         high byte
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] So, with `0036 = 00` and `0037 = 00`, this instruction becomes:
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] JMP ($0036)  →  JMP $0000
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] Then `$0000` contains `BRK` because your freshly allocated RAM is zero-filled.
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] Why the vector is zero
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] Your emulator begins Apple II execution at the ROM’s BASIC entry point:
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] E000: JSR F000
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] That skips the normal Apple II monitor reset/bootstrap sequence. On real hardware, reset begins at
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] the address held in `FFFC/FFFD`, which enters the monitor reset code. That code establishes the
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] zero-page monitor workspace, including the I/O dispatch vectors.
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] In particular, it installs the default character output routine—normally the monitor’s
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] screen-output routine, often called `COUT1`—into `CSWL` at `36/37`.
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] Conceptually, reset performs something equivalent to:
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] CSWL = address of monitor default output routine
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] KSWL = address of monitor default keyboard-input routine
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] where:
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] CSWL = $0036
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] KSWL = $0038
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] The exact routine addresses vary somewhat by Apple II ROM revision, so the correct values should
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] come from the monitor reset path in the ROM image rather than being treated as universal constants.
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] Why it is a vector rather than a direct call
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] The monitor exposes stable public entry points such as:
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] - `COUT` — output the character in `A`
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] - `RDKEY` — read a key
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] - `CROUT` — emit carriage return
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] - `HOME`, `CLEAR`, and similar monitor services
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] Programs call `COUT` at `FDED`, but the actual destination can be replaced by changing `36/$37`.
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] This lets software redirect output to, for example:
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] - the normal text display,
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] - a serial card,
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] - a printer,
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] - a custom terminal routine,
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] - another display mode.
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] So BASIC expects `COUT` to work because the monitor reset code has already assigned `CSWL`.
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] Appropriate emulator startup sequence
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] For the Apple II configuration, emulate a power-on/reset sequence rather than entering directly at
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] BASIC `$E000`.
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] 1. Load RAM and ROMs.
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] 2. Read the reset vector at `FFFC/FFFD`.
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] 3. Set `PC` to that address.
2026-07-15 11:10:47 [gpt-5.6-terra] 4. Execute the monitor reset routine.
