2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] On an original Apple II, those locations are soft-switch addresses. Reading them causes
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] hardware side effects, but the byte returned is generally not a defined switch value.
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] For the addresses in your trace:
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] ╭───────────┬────────────────────────────╮
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] │  Address  │  Read/write side effect    │
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] ├───────────┼────────────────────────────┤
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] │  `$C050`  │  graphics mode             │
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] │  `$C051`  │  text mode                 │
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] │  `$C054`  │  display page 1            │
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] │  `$C055`  │  display page 2            │
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] │  `$C056`  │  low-resolution graphics   │
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] │  `$C057`  │  high-resolution graphics  │
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] ╰───────────┴────────────────────────────╯
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] So:
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] LDA $C056	; select low-res graphics
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] LDA $C054	; select display page 1
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] LDA $C051	; select text mode
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] uses `LDA` only because a memory read is the mechanism that toggles the switch. The
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] accumulator value is not the purpose of the instruction.
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] What value does `LDA $C051` receive?
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] Usually: the floating bus value.
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] The original Apple II does not necessarily drive a deliberate byte onto the CPU data bus for these
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] reads. Instead, the CPU sees whichever bits are present on the shared bus at that moment. This is
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] commonly called the floating bus.
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] Its value can depend on:
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] - the current video scan position,
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] - the byte currently being fetched for display,
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] - screen-memory contents,
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] - precise timing.
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] Therefore, it is not reliably `00`, `FF`, or a documented status byte.
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] In this particular boot code, the values are discarded immediately:
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] FB33  LDA $C056
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] FB36  LDA $C054
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] FB39  LDA $C051
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] FB3C  LDA #$00
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] Thus a simple emulator can safely:
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] 1. apply the soft-switch state transition, and
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] 2. return any stable placeholder such as `$00`.
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] Your current behaviour, returning zero-filled `memory[C0xx]`, is sufficient for this part of boot
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] provided you add the mode side effects. The reset code does not care what was loaded into
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] `A`, because `FB3C` overwrites it with zero.
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] A useful emulation model
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] Keep an `openBus` byte:
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] - Whenever memory or ROM is read, update `openBus` to that byte.
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] - When a soft-switch read has no defined data value, perform its state change and return `openBus`.
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] - For a minimal emulator, simply return `0` instead.
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] The meaningful behaviour is the state change:
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] read $C051 → textMode = true
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] read $C050 → textMode = false
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] read $C054 → page = 1
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] read $C055 → page = 2
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] read $C056 → highResolution = false
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] read $C057 → highResolution = true
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] Strictly, `C056/C057` choose low-res versus high-res graphics; when text mode is selected, the
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] graphics-resolution selection remains stored but does not determine what is visibly displayed.
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] So the concise answer is: real hardware returns an undefined floating-bus byte; the important
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] result of the read is switching video state, not the value placed in `A`.
2026-07-15 11:24:05 [gpt-5.6-terra] 
