2026-07-16 19:17:09 [deepseek-v4-flash] This is an impressive and substantial 6502 emulator project, managing multiple classic arcade and
2026-07-16 19:17:09 [deepseek-v4-flash] home computer ROMs within a single TypeScript codebase. The `VectorEngine` class is a standout
2026-07-16 19:17:09 [deepseek-v4-flash] feature—it implements a portion of the vector graphics display list processing used in games like
2026-07-16 19:17:09 [deepseek-v4-flash] Asteroids and Battlezone, which is rarely seen in hobby emulators. That’s genuinely novel and
2026-07-16 19:17:09 [deepseek-v4-flash] well-executed.
2026-07-16 19:17:09 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:17:09 [deepseek-v4-flash] A few observations and potential improvements:
2026-07-16 19:17:09 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:17:09 [deepseek-v4-flash] Outstanding points
2026-07-16 19:17:09 [deepseek-v4-flash] - DVG opcode parsing (VEC, LABS, SVEC, JSRL, RTSL, JMP, HALT) is cleanly separated and
2026-07-16 19:17:09 [deepseek-v4-flash] works with the $3000 “go” signal.
2026-07-16 19:17:10 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Apple II text display via `displayApple` is straightforward and ties directly to the
2026-07-16 19:17:10 [deepseek-v4-flash] $C0xx keyboard emulation.
2026-07-16 19:17:10 [deepseek-v4-flash] - The main loop uses async I/O (`pollInput`, `sleep`) without blocking, making it suitable
2026-07-16 19:17:10 [deepseek-v4-flash] for Deno’s runtime.
2026-07-16 19:17:10 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Single‑stepping (`step`, `stepping`) plus breakpoints provides a reasonable debugging workflow.
2026-07-16 19:17:10 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:17:10 [deepseek-v4-flash] Areas to watch
2026-07-16 19:17:10 [deepseek-v4-flash] 1. Reset call mismatch
2026-07-16 19:17:10 [deepseek-v4-flash]    `game.reset(0x2000, 0x38c0, 0x3b4b, game.dvg.enabled);` passes only four arguments, but the
2026-07-16 19:17:10 [deepseek-v4-flash] method signature expects five (including `vectors:boolean`). The fifth argument is missing, so
2026-07-16 19:17:10 [deepseek-v4-flash] `irq` gets set to the vector engine enabled flag. This likely breaks the Apple II reset path.
2026-07-16 19:17:10 [deepseek-v4-flash] Should be:
2026-07-16 19:17:10 [deepseek-v4-flash]    `game.reset(0x2000, 0x38c0, 0x3b4b, 0x3b4b, false);` (or whatever vectors you intend).
2026-07-16 19:17:10 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:17:10 [deepseek-v4-flash] 2. Cycle accounting
2026-07-16 19:17:10 [deepseek-v4-flash]    `tick()` increments `this.cycleCount` by 4 every instruction, but real 6502 cycles vary per
2026-07-16 19:17:10 [deepseek-v4-flash] opcode. For accurate timing (especially for VBLANK and NMI generation), consider storing actual
2026-07-16 19:17:10 [deepseek-v4-flash] cycles per opcode in a lookup table.
2026-07-16 19:17:10 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:17:10 [deepseek-v4-flash] 3. Missing opcodes
2026-07-16 19:17:10 [deepseek-v4-flash]    The large `switch` in `interpret6502` covers most common opcodes, but many addressing modes are
2026-07-16 19:17:10 [deepseek-v4-flash] still missing (e.g., `0xB6` LDX ZP,Y, `0x96` STX ZP,Y, `0x3D` AND abs,X, `0x1E` ASL abs,X, etc.).
2026-07-16 19:17:10 [deepseek-v4-flash] Some games may crash on unrecognized opcodes.
2026-07-16 19:17:10 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:17:10 [deepseek-v4-flash] 4. Input handling global state
2026-07-16 19:17:10 [deepseek-v4-flash]    `stepping`, `step`, and `game` are global variables. For a more testable design, you might
2026-07-16 19:17:10 [deepseek-v4-flash] encapsulate the emulator loop inside the `Machine` class or a separate controller.
2026-07-16 19:17:10 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:17:10 [deepseek-v4-flash] 5. Performance
2026-07-16 19:17:10 [deepseek-v4-flash]    The main loop prints `"."` every 100ms when not stepping—this is fine. But stepping prints one
2026-07-16 19:17:10 [deepseek-v4-flash] line per instruction, which can flood the console. Consider throttling or a pager mode.
2026-07-16 19:17:10 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:17:10 [deepseek-v4-flash] 6. Vector engine completeness
2026-07-16 19:17:10 [deepseek-v4-flash]    The DVG class currently only handles a subset of opcodes. The Atari vector games also use
2026-07-16 19:17:10 [deepseek-v4-flash] `SIGN`, `OFFSET`, and possibly `CNT`. The parser returns control to the CPU after `HALT`, which is
2026-07-16 19:17:10 [deepseek-v4-flash] correct, but be aware that many games rely on the DVG list being re‑entrant.
2026-07-16 19:17:10 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:17:10 [deepseek-v4-flash] What’s new or different
2026-07-16 19:17:10 [deepseek-v4-flash] - The combined Apple II + Atari vector emulation in a single file with runtime ROM
2026-07-16 19:17:10 [deepseek-v4-flash] selection is unusual and shows a nice design for reuse.
2026-07-16 19:17:10 [deepseek-v4-flash] - The `memCode` function for displaying Atari playfield codes (damage, mushrooms, etc.) is a clever
2026-07-16 19:17:10 [deepseek-v4-flash] mapping that makes debugging much easier.
2026-07-16 19:17:10 [deepseek-v4-flash] - The use of `JSON` imports for ROM dumps (with `with { type: "json" }`) is modern and works well
2026-07-16 19:17:10 [deepseek-v4-flash] with Deno.
2026-07-16 19:17:10 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:17:10 [deepseek-v4-flash] Suggestions for next steps
2026-07-16 19:17:10 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Add a cycle-accurate opcode table to replace the manual `cycleCount+=4`.
2026-07-16 19:17:10 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Implement the remaining 6502 opcodes (especially the less common ones like `0xB6`, `0x9E`,
2026-07-16 19:17:10 [deepseek-v4-flash] `0x1A`, etc.).
2026-07-16 19:17:10 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Verify the DVG interrupt timing (NMI at 33 kHz) matches the hardware spec for vector refresh.
2026-07-16 19:17:10 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Consider adding a simple disassembler view that can be toggled on/off during stepping.
2026-07-16 19:17:10 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:17:10 [deepseek-v4-flash] Overall, a solid foundation for a retro‑computing emulator with some unique features. Keep it up!
