2026-07-16 19:21:21 [deepseek-v4-flash] The backspace key behavior is typical of Apple II emulation. The issue is that the emulator pushes
2026-07-16 19:21:21 [deepseek-v4-flash] the raw keycode `8` (which is indeed the Apple II keyboard code for backspace) into the game's key
2026-07-16 19:21:21 [deepseek-v4-flash] buffer via `game.pushKey(8)`. The game's keyboard‑read routine (typically polling `$C000` for
2026-07-16 19:21:21 [deepseek-v4-flash] keypresses) will then fetch that value. Many Apple II programs, especially games, do not
2026-07-16 19:21:21 [deepseek-v4-flash] interpret the backspace character as a cursor‑movement/erase command—instead, they treat it as a
2026-07-16 19:21:21 [deepseek-v4-flash] printable character and write it directly into the text screen (or use it in a numeric input
2026-07-16 19:21:21 [deepseek-v4-flash] routine).
2026-07-16 19:21:21 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:21:21 [deepseek-v4-flash] Why “odd characters” appear
2026-07-16 19:21:21 [deepseek-v4-flash] In the Apple II character set, code `08` corresponds to a graphic symbol (usually a small square or
2026-07-16 19:21:21 [deepseek-v4-flash] a blank) because the ROM font maps many control codes to glyphs. When the game stores `08` into
2026-07-16 19:21:21 [deepseek-v4-flash] screen RAM, that symbol appears. The backspace “works” in the sense that the game decrements the
2026-07-16 19:21:21 [deepseek-v4-flash] cursor position or deletes a previous character, but then it also writes that control character
2026-07-16 19:21:21 [deepseek-v4-flash] into the screen buffer, leaving a visible artifact.
2026-07-16 19:21:21 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:21:21 [deepseek-v4-flash] How to fix it
2026-07-16 19:21:21 [deepseek-v4-flash] There are two common approaches:
2026-07-16 19:21:21 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:21:21 [deepseek-v4-flash] 1. Translate the backspace key to a space at the previous screen position
2026-07-16 19:21:21 [deepseek-v4-flash]    Instead of pushing raw keycode `8`, you can simulate the backspace effect by directly modifying
2026-07-16 19:21:21 [deepseek-v4-flash] the screen memory. For example, when a backspace is received, you could:
2026-07-16 19:21:21 [deepseek-v4-flash]    - Decrement the cursor position (if the game uses a memory‑based cursor variable—but that’s
2026-07-16 19:21:21 [deepseek-v4-flash] game‑dependent).
2026-07-16 19:21:21 [deepseek-v4-flash]    - Write a space (`0x20 | 0x80` for normal display) at that location.
2026-07-16 19:21:21 [deepseek-v4-flash]    - Then push nothing (or a dummy key) so the game doesn’t try to read it.
2026-07-16 19:21:21 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:21:21 [deepseek-v4-flash]    This is fragile because games manage their own cursor pointers.
2026-07-16 19:21:21 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:21:21 [deepseek-v4-flash] 2. Use the correct key mapping expected by the game
2026-07-16 19:21:21 [deepseek-v4-flash]    Many Apple II games expect Clr (clear) or Delete (`FF`) to erase the character
2026-07-16 19:21:21 [deepseek-v4-flash] left of the cursor, while backspace (`08`) is often reserved for the firmware’s line‑editing
2026-07-16 19:21:21 [deepseek-v4-flash] routines. If you map Delete (keycode 127 on modern keyboards) to `FF` and backspace (key 8) to
2026-07-16 19:21:21 [deepseek-v4-flash] `08`, the game might treat it as a regular character.
2026-07-16 19:21:21 [deepseek-v4-flash]    - Check the game’s source code (if available) or experiment: try pushing `00`, `7F`, or `$DF`
2026-07-16 19:21:21 [deepseek-v4-flash] (left‑arrow) instead.
2026-07-16 19:21:21 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:21:21 [deepseek-v4-flash] Suggested modification to your code
2026-07-16 19:21:21 [deepseek-v4-flash] In the `runMachine` loop, you can change the backspace handling:
2026-07-16 19:21:21 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:21:21 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
