2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] performs a screen erase) is not running during the monitor’s input loop in your ROM, or the
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] monitor’s backspace handling is being bypassed by the emulator’s direct memory display. Instead,
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] the character code is written straight to screen memory, causing a glyph to appear.
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] Why this happens
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] - When you type in the monitor, it reads a key (`$C000`), then calls `COUT` to display it.
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] - `COUT` (at `FDF0` in standard Apple II firmware) checks for `08` (backspace): it decrements the
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] cursor pointer, writes a space, then decrements again. However, the monitor’s input routine
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] also immediately stores the character to screen memory before calling `COUT` on some ROM variants.
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] Or your ROM might use a different routine that doesn’t call `COUT` for control characters.
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] - The `displayApple` function reads raw screen memory, so any byte written to `400` shows up,
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] including the glyph for `08`.
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] A direct emulator‑side fix
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] Instead of fighting the ROM’s behavior, you can simulate backspace at the emulator level by
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] directly manipulating the Apple II’s text cursor and screen memory. This makes the monitor (and any
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] program that uses the standard cursor at `24`/`25`) work correctly without changing key mappings.
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] 1. Add this helper to your `Machine` class
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] backspace() {
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash]   // Apple II text cursor location: low byte at $24, high byte at $25
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash]   const cursorLow = this.memory[0x24];
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash]   const cursorHigh = this.memory[0x25];
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash]   if (cursorLow === 0 && cursorHigh === 0x04) return; // top‑left, nothing to erase
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash]   // Compute previous cursor position
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash]   let newPos = (cursorHigh << 8) | cursorLow;
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash]   newPos--;
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash]   if (newPos < 0x0400) return; // before start of text page1
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash]   // Write a space (0x20 | 0x80 = normal display) at the previous position
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash]   this.memory[newPos] = 0xA0;  // 0xA0 = space with high bit set (normal mode)
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash]   // Update cursor pointers
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash]   this.memory[0x24] = newPos & 0xFF;
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash]   this.memory[0x25] = (newPos >> 8) & 0xFF;
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] }
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] 2. In your main loop, replace the backspace handling
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] case 127:   // terminal backspace
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash]   game.backspace();
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash]   // Do NOT push any key – the game will not see it, but the screen is updated.
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash]   break;
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] 3. Also consider clearing the keyboard strobe manually
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] The monitor might be waiting for a key to clear the strobe (`C010`). To avoid hanging, you can also
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] set the memory at `C010` to be read by the monitor’s loop. But for the hex monitor, this hack works
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] because the monitor is echoing characters, and backspace is not a character to echo.
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] Test
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Enter the monitor (e.g., by pressing `Ctrl+B` or `Reset`).
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Type some hex digits, then press Backspace. The last character should be erased, and no glyph
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] appears.
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] If the cursor addresses differ
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] Some games or modified monitors store the cursor at different addresses. Check the ROM’s labels for
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] `CURSOR` or `CH`. If you can’t find them, set a breakpoint at the address where the monitor reads
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] `C000` and look for writes to memory near `24`–`$25`. This direct approach is more reliable than
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] relying on the ROM’s own backspace handling.
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] Let me know if you need help locating the correct cursor variables in your specific ROM.
2026-07-16 19:31:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:31:46 [simon@midnightblue.local] instead of lower case does apple2 show inverse of uppercase?
2026-07-16 19:31:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 1.9.5 deepseek-v4-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.004 82.45KB 10.19s
2026-07-16 19:31:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] Yes, the original Apple II and Apple II+ (which your emulator likely targets) had no lowercase
2026-07-16 19:31:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] letters in hardware. Instead, they used inverse uppercase (white on black) by setting bit 7
2026-07-16 19:31:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] of the character code. The standard character set maps codes `00`–`7F` to normal
2026-07-16 19:31:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] uppercase/graphics, while `80`–`FF` show the inverse (and sometimes flashing) version of
2026-07-16 19:31:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] those same characters.
2026-07-16 19:31:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:31:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] Your `displayApple` function masks the byte with `& 0x7f`, stripping the high bit. So any byte with
2026-07-16 19:31:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] bit 7 set (e.g., `C1` for inverse "A") becomes `41` and displays as normal "A". That’s fine for
2026-07-16 19:31:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] normal text, but when the monitor writes a raw backspace code (`08`), it has bit 7 clear, and
2026-07-16 19:31:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] masking still gives `08`—a control character that appears as a glyph (like a small square or
2026-07-16 19:31:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] underscore).
2026-07-16 19:31:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:31:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] If you want inverse uppercase to appear as actual visual alternates, you’d need a display routine
2026-07-16 19:31:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] that respects bit 7 (e.g., drawing with a background color). But most home‑brewn Apple II emulators
2026-07-16 19:31:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] just treat the high bit as “inverted” and render accordingly.
2026-07-16 19:31:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:31:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] In short: Yes, to get a lowercase‑like appearance, programmers used inverse uppercase, but
2026-07-16 19:31:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] your current display function ignores the inverse flag.
2026-07-16 19:31:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
