2026-07-16 19:35:23 [simon@midnightblue.local] hmmm,most bytes have top bit set, for original apple2 rom how should i interpret?
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 1.9.5 deepseek-v4-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.005 84.42KB 21.50s
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] On an original Apple II (II/II+), the video memory at `$400` stores 7‑bit character codes
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] with an 8th bit (bit 7) that toggles the display mode:
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Bit 7 = 0 → Normal (white on black)
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Bit 7 = 1 → Inverse (black on white)
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] Most system software (including the monitor) writes bytes with bit 7 set by default,
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] because the inverse mode gives better contrast on many monitors. The character itself (bits 0–6) is
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] the standard Apple II character code (uppercase letters, numbers, punctuation, and graphic
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] symbols).
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] Your current `displayApple` does:
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] let charCode = (byte & 0x7f) || 32;
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] This strips bit 7 and then treats the code as a plain ASCII/Apple II character. Because the high
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] bit is lost, everything appears as normal text, and you never see the intended inverse
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] display (e.g., the monitor prompt, cursor, etc.). The backspace code `08` has bit 7 clear, so after
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] masking it stays `08` and prints a glyph.
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] To properly emulate the Apple II text screen:
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] 1. Use `byte & 0x7F` to get the character code.
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] 2. Use `byte & 0x80` as an inverse flag.
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] 3. Render inverse characters by using a terminal escape sequence (e.g., ANSI reverse video)
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] or displaying the character in a different color.
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] For example, modify `displayApple`:
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] function displayApple(memory: Uint8Array){
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash]     console.log(cursorHome + cursorDown);
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash]     for (let y = 0; y < 24; y++) {
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash]         let line = [];
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash]         let offset = 0x400 + 40 * (y >> 3) + 128 * (y & 7); // correct Apple II row mapping
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash]         for (let x = 0; x < 40; x++) {
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash]             let byte = memory[offset + x];
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash]             let charCode = byte & 0x7F;
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash]             if (charCode === 0) charCode = 32; // null → space
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash]             let char = String.fromCharCode(charCode);
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash]             if (byte & 0x80) {
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash]                 // Inverse: wrap with ANSI reverse video
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash]                 char = "\x1b[7m" + char + "\x1b[27m";
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash]             }
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash]             line.push(char);
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash]         }
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash]         console.log(line.join(""));
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash]     }
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] }
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] Now the monitor’s prompt (usually a blinking inverse `]`) and any backspace erasures will appear as
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] intended (inverse characters). The backspace `08` will still be printed as a glyph, because the
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] monitor’s `COUT` routine normally intercepts `08` and erases the previous character—but your
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] emulator is not calling `COUT`; it’s reading raw screen memory. However, with this fix, the
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] backspace glyph will at least show as an inverse symbol (which is still not ideal, but you can see
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] that the high bit is being respected).
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] Additional note
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] Your current `offset` formula for the Apple II text page is correct for the original
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] 40‑column layout:
2026-07-16 19:35:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] `0x400 + 40  (row >> 3) + 128  (row & 7)`
2026-07-16 19:35:47 [deepseek-v4-flash] That’s the standard Apple II “mixed” mode mapping. Good.
2026-07-16 19:35:47 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:35:47 [deepseek-v4-flash] For a complete emulator
2026-07-16 19:35:47 [deepseek-v4-flash] You may also want to handle the flashing mode (when the strobe at `$C050` selects flashing
2026-07-16 19:35:47 [deepseek-v4-flash] instead of inverse). But for basic hex monitor use, inverse rendering is enough.
2026-07-16 19:35:47 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:35:47 [deepseek-v4-flash] Let me know if you want a version that also supports the full Apple II character set (including
2026-07-16 19:35:47 [deepseek-v4-flash] control code glyphs).
