2026-07-16 19:39:26 [simon@midnightblue.local] why would 0 go to p when cursor is on it?
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 1.9.5 deepseek-v4-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.005 87.82KB 33.86s
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] It sounds like the hex digit `0` is being displayed as the letter `P` when you type it. This
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] indicates a mismatch between the keycode your emulator sends and the code the Apple II
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] monitor expects.
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] Most common cause: you are sending a scan code instead of an ASCII code
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] The original Apple II keyboard does not produce ASCII codes directly. It returns a
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] key‑matrix scan code (a 7‑bit value) with bit 7 set. The firmware (at `$FD18` or similar)
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] then translates that scan code into the ASCII character that appears on screen.
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] If your emulator bypasses that translation (i.e., you call `pushKey` with an ASCII value like
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] `0x30` for `0`), then the monitor will see that ASCII value as if it were a scan code. The
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] ASCII `0x30` happens to be the scan code for the `P` key on the Apple II keyboard (key position
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] `$30` = `P` on the original matrix). So the monitor displays a `P`.
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] How to fix
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] You have two options:
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] Option 1: Let the firmware translate (recommended)
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] Instead of sending raw ASCII codes, send the Apple II scan codes for the keys. For example:
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] - `0` key → scan code `0B` (or `8B` with strobe)
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] - `1` key → scan code `$0C`
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] - `2` key → scan code `$0D`
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] - etc.
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] You can find the full scan‑code table for the Apple II keyboard. Then the monitor’s own ROM routine
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] will convert them to the correct ASCII characters and your problem disappears.
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] Option 2: Directly write to screen memory
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] If you want to keep sending ASCII codes, you must also bypass the monitor’s input handler and
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] directly place the character into screen RAM at the cursor position. That’s more work and fragile.
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] Quick diagnosis: add a debug print
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] Add this temporary logging in `readAddress` at `$C000`:
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] case 0xc000:
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash]   let key = this.keyState;
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash]   if (((key & 0x80) == 0) && this.keyBuffer.length) {
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash]     key = this.keyBuffer.shift() | 0x80;
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash]     this.keyState = key;
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash]     console.log(`[KBD] reading $C000 → 0x${key.toString(16)}`);
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash]   }
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash]   return key;
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] Then press `0` and look at the console.
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] - If you see `0xB0` (ASCII `0` with bit 7 set), the monitor should display an inverse `0`. If it
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] shows `P` instead, the monitor is interpreting `0xB0` as a scan code (which equals the key for `P`
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] on the Apple II).
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] - If you see a different value (e.g., `0x0B`), you are already sending a scan code, but maybe the
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] wrong one.
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] Recommended fix for Option 1
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] Change your key mapping to send Apple II scan codes:
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] // Example mapping for hex digits (scan codes for Apple II)
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash] const keyScanCodes = {
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash]   '0': 0x0B,
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash]   '1': 0x0C,
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash]   '2': 0x0D,
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash]   '3': 0x0E,
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash]   '4': 0x0F,
2026-07-16 19:40:00 [deepseek-v4-flash]   '5': 0x10,
