2026-07-17 03:41:41 [kimi-k3] Thanks for sharing — this is a substantial update. A few comments, roughly grouped:
2026-07-17 03:41:41 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-17 03:41:41 [kimi-k3] What's new or notable
2026-07-17 03:41:41 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-17 03:41:41 [kimi-k3] - Apple II is now the live target (`gameRom=apple2`), with a proper text-page renderer. The row
2026-07-17 03:41:41 [kimi-k3] interleave in `displayApple` (`0x400 + 40(y>>3) + 128(y&7)`) matches real text page 1
2026-07-17 03:41:41 [kimi-k3] layout exactly.
2026-07-17 03:41:41 [kimi-k3] - Keyboard handling at `C000`/`C010` follows hardware convention — strobe bit set on read when a
2026-07-17 03:41:41 [kimi-k3] key is pending, cleared by any access to `C010`. Speaker toggle stub at `C030` is in place.
2026-07-17 03:41:41 [kimi-k3] - The DVG opcode map in `VectorEngine` (LABS/VEC/SVEC/JSRL/RTSL/HALT at 0xA–0xF) matches the Atari
2026-07-17 03:41:41 [kimi-k3] vector generator, and `JMP Indirect` (0x6C) faithfully reproduces the original 6502 page-boundary
2026-07-17 03:41:41 [kimi-k3] wrap bug — a nice authenticity touch.
2026-07-17 03:41:41 [kimi-k3] - BCD paths in `adc`/`sbc` are present, and the `sbc` borrow/overflow derivation looks correct.
2026-07-17 03:41:41 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-17 03:41:41 [kimi-k3] Possible issues
2026-07-17 03:41:41 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-17 03:41:41 [kimi-k3] 1. `reportMissing` prints wrong values. `Object.keys(missingCodes)` returns strings, and
2026-07-17 03:41:41 [kimi-k3] `String.toString(16)` ignores the radix, so `hex(code, 2)` echoes the decimal key rather than
2026-07-17 03:41:41 [kimi-k3] converting it. A `parseInt(code, 10)` before formatting would fix it.
2026-07-17 03:41:41 [kimi-k3] 2. Inverse video polarity in `displayApple`. On real hardware, bit 7 set (80–FF) is normal
2026-07-17 03:41:41 [kimi-k3] text; 00–3F is inverse and 40–7F flashes. The current code renders bit-7-set as inverse, which is
2026-07-17 03:41:41 [kimi-k3] inverted relative to hardware — unless that's a deliberate aesthetic choice.
2026-07-17 03:41:41 [kimi-k3] 3. `interrupt()` clears `stepping`. Any taken interrupt — including the first vblank IRQ
2026-07-17 03:41:41 [kimi-k3] after a CLI — halts the machine. Handy as a debug trap, but it will stop raster-mode execution the
2026-07-17 03:41:41 [kimi-k3] moment the ROM enables interrupts. Worth confirming that's still intended.
2026-07-17 03:41:41 [kimi-k3] 4. Inconsistent write paths. `INC Absolute` (0xEE), `DEC Absolute` (0xCE), and `LSR
2026-07-17 03:41:41 [kimi-k3] Absolute` (0x4E) write via `this.memory[address]=` directly, bypassing `writeAddress` and therefore
2026-07-17 03:41:41 [kimi-k3] the screen-RAM dirty flag — unlike their X-indexed counterparts, which do route through
2026-07-17 03:41:41 [kimi-k3] `writeAddress`. An `INC $4xx` would silently miss a redraw.
2026-07-17 03:41:41 [kimi-k3] 5. Swapped comments on key handlers. `case 100` is lowercase 'd' and `case 68` is uppercase
2026-07-17 03:41:41 [kimi-k3] 'D' — the `//D` / `//d` comments are reversed.
2026-07-17 03:41:41 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-17 03:41:41 [kimi-k3] Housekeeping observations
2026-07-17 03:41:41 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-17 03:41:41 [kimi-k3] - `updatePumps`/`fadePumps`/`pump` appear to be leftover thruster code — nothing calls them now.
2026-07-17 03:41:41 [kimi-k3] - `select` is imported but unused since ROM selection is commented out.
2026-07-17 03:41:41 [kimi-k3] - The `""+` prefix in `hex()` is redundant.
2026-07-17 03:41:41 [kimi-k3] - `missingCodes`, `keyBuffer`, and a few parameters (`keyCode`, `offset`) are untyped; the rest of
2026-07-17 03:41:41 [kimi-k3] the file is fairly well annotated, so these stand out.
2026-07-17 03:41:41 [kimi-k3] - BRK halting the machine reads as a deliberate debug breakpoint — fine, just noting it alongside
2026-07-17 03:41:41 [kimi-k3] the interrupt behaviour above.
2026-07-17 03:41:41 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-17 03:41:41 [kimi-k3] The fixed 4-cycles-per-instruction placeholder in `tick()` is clearly marked, so I assume accurate
2026-07-17 03:41:41 [kimi-k3] cycle counting is on the roadmap. Overall the structure is holding up well for a dual-architecture
2026-07-17 03:41:41 [kimi-k3] (vector/raster) emulator — the main thing I'd watch is the Machine class accumulating game-specific
2026-07-17 03:41:41 [kimi-k3] behaviour (the Centipede sprite overlay in `refreshCRT`, Apple II soft switches) as more titles
2026-07-17 03:41:41 [kimi-k3] come online.
