2026-07-19 20:19:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] 🟠nitrologic Relay 1.9.7 kimi-k2.7-code 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.002 497B 11.25s
2026-07-19 20:19:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] On the 6502, opcode $41 (decimal 65) is:
2026-07-19 20:19:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-07-19 20:19:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] • `EOR ($nn, X)`*
2026-07-19 20:19:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] • EOR with indirect, X* addressing.
2026-07-19 20:19:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-07-19 20:19:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] - Bytes: 2
2026-07-19 20:19:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] - Cycles: 6
2026-07-19 20:19:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] - Operation: `A ← A XOR M`
2026-07-19 20:19:34 [kimi-k2.7-code]   - Fetch a zero-page pointer from `(operand + X)` (wrapped in zero page).
2026-07-19 20:19:34 [kimi-k2.7-code]   - Read the byte at that 16-bit address.
2026-07-19 20:19:34 [kimi-k2.7-code]   - XOR it with the accumulator.
2026-07-19 20:19:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-07-19 20:19:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] - Flags affected: N, Z
2026-07-19 20:19:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-07-19 20:19:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] It is not to be confused with `$41` on some other 8-bit CPUs; on a standard 6502 it is always `EOR
2026-07-19 20:19:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] (indirect,X)`.
2026-07-19 20:19:34 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-07-19 20:20:49 [simon@midnightblue.local] implementation pls
2026-07-19 20:21:23 [kimi-k2.7-code] 🟠nitrologic Relay 1.9.7 kimi-k2.7-code 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.022 71.06KB 32.15s
2026-07-19 20:21:23 [kimi-k2.7-code] Here is the implementation for opcode `$41` (`EOR (indirect, X)`). Place it near the other
2026-07-19 20:21:23 [kimi-k2.7-code] `(Indirect, X)` or `EOR` cases in `interpret6502`.
2026-07-19 20:21:23 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-07-19 20:21:23 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-07-19 20:21:23 [kimi-k2.7-code] case 0x41: // EOR (Indirect, X)
2026-07-19 20:21:23 [kimi-k2.7-code] 	{
2026-07-19 20:21:23 [kimi-k2.7-code] 		const base = this.get8(pc + 1);
2026-07-19 20:21:23 [kimi-k2.7-code] 		const pointer = (base + this.registers[register.X]) & 0xFF;
2026-07-19 20:21:23 [kimi-k2.7-code] 		const addrLow = this.memory[pointer];
2026-07-19 20:21:23 [kimi-k2.7-code] 		const addrHigh = this.memory[(pointer + 1) & 0xFF];
2026-07-19 20:21:23 [kimi-k2.7-code] 		const address = (addrHigh << 8) | addrLow;
2026-07-19 20:21:23 [kimi-k2.7-code] 		const value = this.readAddress(address);
2026-07-19 20:21:23 [kimi-k2.7-code] 		this.registers[register.A] ^= value;
2026-07-19 20:21:23 [kimi-k2.7-code] 		this.setStatus(this.registers[register.A]);
2026-07-19 20:21:23 [kimi-k2.7-code] 		this.step(2);
2026-07-19 20:21:23 [kimi-k2.7-code] 		return "EOR\t($" + hex(base) + ",X)";
2026-07-19 20:21:23 [kimi-k2.7-code] 	}
2026-07-19 20:21:23 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-07-19 20:21:23 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
2026-07-19 20:21:23 [kimi-k2.7-code] Notes on behaviour:
2026-07-19 20:21:23 [kimi-k2.7-code] - `base + X` wraps within the zero page (`& 0xFF`).
2026-07-19 20:21:23 [kimi-k2.7-code] - The indirect pointer is formed from two consecutive zero-page bytes, with the second wrapping in
2026-07-19 20:21:23 [kimi-k2.7-code] zero page (`(pointer + 1) & 0xFF`).
2026-07-19 20:21:23 [kimi-k2.7-code] - The final address is a 16-bit absolute address, so it is read through `readAddress` for I/O
2026-07-19 20:21:23 [kimi-k2.7-code] interception consistency with your `LDA (Indirect, X)` implementation.
2026-07-19 20:21:23 [kimi-k2.7-code] 
