2026-07-22 14:30:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] While opinions vary on an album as wildly creative as Brian Eno and David Byrne's 1981 masterpiece
2026-07-22 14:30:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] My Life in the Bush of Ghosts, many listeners and critics consider the track "The
2026-07-22 14:30:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] Jezebel Spirit" to feature the most intense and unusual sample.
2026-07-22 14:30:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-07-22 14:30:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] The sample consists of audio from an actual live radio broadcast featuring an unnamed exorcist
2026-07-22 14:30:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] attempting to cast out spirits from a woman. Hearing an intense, dramatic exorcism ("I command the
2026-07-22 14:30:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] Jezebel spirit to come out of her right now!") laid over a complex, syncopated funk beat creates a
2026-07-22 14:30:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] deeply bizarre and captivating contrast.
2026-07-22 14:30:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-07-22 14:30:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] A few other tracks feature particularly striking samples worth noting:
2026-07-22 14:30:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-07-22 14:30:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] • "America Is Waiting": Features aggressive, cross-talking rants from San Francisco talk
2026-07-22 14:30:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] radio host Ray Taliaferro, capturing the intense paranoia and noise of American political radio.
2026-07-22 14:30:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] • "Help Me Somebody": Built around a fiery, rhythmic sermon by the New Orleans evangelist
2026-07-22 14:30:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] Reverend Paul Morton, whose vocal cadences match the music so naturally it feels like a rehearsed
2026-07-22 14:30:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] performance.
2026-07-22 14:30:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] • "Qu'ran": Featured on original pressings, this track used a recording of Islamic Quranic
2026-07-22 14:30:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] recitations over a driving beat. It was eventually removed from later reissues out of respect for
2026-07-22 14:30:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] religious sensitivities after requests from the Islamic Council of Great Britain.
2026-07-22 14:30:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-07-22 14:30:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] The track that stands out most often for sheer eccentricity, however, remains the eerie exorcism on
2026-07-22 14:30:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] "The Jezebel Spirit."
2026-07-22 14:30:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-07-22 14:33:51 [simon@midnightblue.local] explain angel sloppification in musical terms
2026-07-22 14:34:01 [gemini-3.6-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 1.9.8 gemini-3.6-flash 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.010 3.246KB 9.52s
2026-07-22 14:34:01 [gemini-3.6-flash] In musical terms, "angel sloppification" can be understood as the deliberate introduction
2026-07-22 14:34:01 [gemini-3.6-flash] of human imperfection, pitch instability, and temporal looseness into pristine, ethereal, or
2026-07-22 14:34:01 [gemini-3.6-flash] "celestial" musical textures.
2026-07-22 14:34:01 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-07-22 14:34:01 [gemini-3.6-flash] It takes soundscapes that are traditionally clean, polished, and heavenly (the "angel" aspect) and
2026-07-22 14:34:01 [gemini-3.6-flash] introduces organic decay, microtonal drift, or relaxed execution (the "sloppification" aspect).
2026-07-22 14:34:01 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-07-22 14:34:01 [gemini-3.6-flash] Here is how that concept breaks down across specific musical dimensions:
2026-07-22 14:34:01 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-07-22 14:34:01 [gemini-3.6-flash] 1. Pitch and Intonation (Microtonality & Drift)
2026-07-22 14:34:01 [gemini-3.6-flash] • Pitch Instability: Instead of mathematically pure intonation or strict equal temperament,
2026-07-22 14:34:01 [gemini-3.6-flash] pitch drift is introduced using wow and flutter, tape saturation, or subtle pitch-bend modulation.
2026-07-22 14:34:01 [gemini-3.6-flash] • Detuned Voicings: Ethereal extended chords (such as Major 9ths, Major 7#11s, or
2026-07-22 14:34:01 [gemini-3.6-flash] open-fifth choir pads) are slightly detuned against one another by a few cents. This creates
2026-07-22 14:34:01 [gemini-3.6-flash] acoustic beating and warmth rather than sterile perfection.
2026-07-22 14:34:01 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-07-22 14:34:01 [gemini-3.6-flash] 2. Rhythm and Micro-Timing (The "Drunken" or Loose Grid)
2026-07-22 14:34:01 [gemini-3.6-flash] • Unquantized Phrasing: Stripping away rigid quantization in favor of rubato or humanized
2026-07-22 14:34:01 [gemini-3.6-flash] micro-timing. Notes arrive slightly ahead of or behind the pulse (often associated with swing or
2026-07-22 14:34:01 [gemini-3.6-flash] "behind-the-beat" feel).
2026-07-22 14:34:01 [gemini-3.6-flash] • Asynchronous Layers: Multiple celestial sound sources (e.g., harp arpeggios, ambient
2026-07-22 14:34:01 [gemini-3.6-flash] synth pads, and vocal textures) run at slightly conflicting tempos, creating a rolling, overlapping
2026-07-22 14:34:01 [gemini-3.6-flash] swell rather than a synchronized rhythm.
2026-07-22 14:34:01 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-07-22 14:34:01 [gemini-3.6-flash] 3. Timbre and Production (Lo-Fi Degradation)
2026-07-22 14:34:01 [gemini-3.6-flash] • High-End Softening: Lush, high-frequency reverberation tails are processed through
2026-07-22 14:34:01 [gemini-3.6-flash] low-pass filters, soft clipping, or analog tape emulation to dull the digital sheen.
2026-07-22 14:34:01 [gemini-3.6-flash] • Artifacts as Texture: Intentionally retaining dynamic imperfections, breath noises, pedal
2026-07-22 14:34:01 [gemini-3.6-flash] thumps, or vinyl crackle alongside pristine choral or orchestral voices.
2026-07-22 14:34:01 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-07-22 14:34:01 [gemini-3.6-flash] 4. Performance Dynamics (Lazy Articulation)
2026-07-22 14:34:02 [gemini-3.6-flash] • Slurred Transitions: Using heavy portamento or glissando between lush harmonic voicings
2026-07-22 14:34:02 [gemini-3.6-flash] rather than crisp, distinct chord changes.
2026-07-22 14:34:02 [gemini-3.6-flash] • Uneven Velocity: Allowing note attacks within a chord to strike at irregular velocities,
2026-07-22 14:34:02 [gemini-3.6-flash] giving the impression of an performer leaning casually into a passage.
2026-07-22 14:34:02 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-07-22 14:34:02 [gemini-3.6-flash] In short, it is the art of making divine, high-register harmonies feel warm, worn, and humanly
2026-07-22 14:34:02 [gemini-3.6-flash] imperfect.
2026-07-22 14:34:02 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-07-22 14:36:52 [simon@midnightblue.local] again but please ground your answer in some kind of reality
2026-07-22 14:37:02 [gemini-3.6-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 1.9.8 gemini-3.6-flash 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.013 5.886KB 9.47s
2026-07-22 14:37:02 [gemini-3.6-flash] To ground this in reality: "angel sloppification" is not a formal music theory term. It is
2026-07-22 14:37:02 [gemini-3.6-flash] informal internet slang used in music discussions to describe taking clean, beautiful, or ethereal
2026-07-22 14:37:02 [gemini-3.6-flash] music ("angelic") and deliberately making it loose, pitch-bent, degraded, or unpolished ("sloppy").
2026-07-22 14:37:02 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-07-22 14:37:02 [gemini-3.6-flash] Instead of theoretical concepts, here is how this actually shows up in real-world musical genres
2026-07-22 14:37:02 [gemini-3.6-flash] and production techniques:
2026-07-22 14:37:02 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-07-22 14:37:02 [gemini-3.6-flash] 1. "Slowed + Reverb" & Chopped and Screwed
2026-07-22 14:37:02 [gemini-3.6-flash] • The Reality: Creators take pristine pop or R&B vocals (like Ariana Grande or Frank
2026-07-22 14:37:02 [gemini-3.6-flash] Ocean), slow the track down by 15–20%, pitch the audio down, and drench it in massive reverb.
2026-07-22 14:37:02 [gemini-3.6-flash] • The Effect: A polished, high-pitched vocal becomes a sluggish, druggy, and melancholic
2026-07-22 14:37:02 [gemini-3.6-flash] murmur while retaining its original lush harmonies.
2026-07-22 14:37:02 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-07-22 14:37:02 [gemini-3.6-flash] 2. Shoegaze and Dream Pop
2026-07-22 14:37:02 [gemini-3.6-flash] • The Reality: Bands like My Bloody Valentine or Cocteau Twins record
2026-07-22 14:37:02 [gemini-3.6-flash] beautiful, soaring vocal melodies and ethereal chord progressions, but then run them through bent
2026-07-22 14:37:02 [gemini-3.6-flash] whammy bars ("glide guitar"), heavy distortion, and layers of reverse reverb.
2026-07-22 14:37:02 [gemini-3.6-flash] • The Effect: The underlying music is heavenly, but the execution sounds blurry, warped,
2026-07-22 14:37:02 [gemini-3.6-flash] and deliberately unsteady.
2026-07-22 14:37:02 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-07-22 14:37:02 [gemini-3.6-flash] 3. Lo-Fi Hip Hop and "Drunk Timing"
2026-07-22 14:37:02 [gemini-3.6-flash] • The Reality: Producers like J Dilla or Knxwledge take pristine jazz or
2026-07-22 14:37:02 [gemini-3.6-flash] soul samples (lush piano chords, smooth vocal hooks) and intentionally turn off the rhythmic grid
2026-07-22 14:37:02 [gemini-3.6-flash] on their sequencers. They play the drums slightly off-beat and apply heavy tape wobble (wow and
2026-07-22 14:37:02 [gemini-3.6-flash] flutter).
2026-07-22 14:37:02 [gemini-3.6-flash] • The Effect: The sample remains gorgeous, but the timing feels intentionally loose,
2026-07-22 14:37:02 [gemini-3.6-flash] stumbling, and "sloppy."
2026-07-22 14:37:02 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-07-22 14:37:02 [gemini-3.6-flash] 4. Ambient Tape Decay
2026-07-22 14:37:02 [gemini-3.6-flash] • The Reality: Electronic artists like William Basinski (The Disintegration
2026-07-22 14:37:02 [gemini-3.6-flash] Loops) or Board of Canada take pastoral, hymn-like horn or choral samples and run them
2026-07-22 14:37:02 [gemini-3.6-flash] through old cassette decks or decaying magnetic tape.
2026-07-22 14:37:02 [gemini-3.6-flash] • The Effect: Bright, angelic passages gradually drop pitch, crackle, drop out, and warp as
2026-07-22 14:37:02 [gemini-3.6-flash] the tape mechanically fails.
2026-07-22 14:37:02 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-07-22 14:37:02 [gemini-3.6-flash] In short, in actual music culture, it refers to the practice of taking something pure or
2026-07-22 14:37:02 [gemini-3.6-flash] hyper-produced and running it through lo-fi, off-kilter, or decelerated processing to give it a
2026-07-22 14:37:02 [gemini-3.6-flash] worn, human, or haunting feel.
2026-07-22 14:37:02 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-07-22 19:25:38 [simon@midnightblue.local] with applesoft roms i want emulate a soft reset in my emu
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 1.9.9 gemini-3.6-flash 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.016 536B 13.18s
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] To emulate a soft reset (equivalent to pressing Ctrl+Reset on a physical Apple II) with Applesoft
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] ROMs, you need to replicate how the Apple II System Monitor and memory vectors handle the reset
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] sequence.
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] Key Memory Locations
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] • `FFFC` / `FFFD`: 6502 Hardware Reset Vector. Points to the System Monitor reset handler,
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] typically `$FA62`.
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] • `03F2` / `03F3` (`SOFTEV`): Soft Reset Vector (Low/High byte). Holds the target memory
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] address to jump to on a soft reset.
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] • `$03F4` (`POWERUP`): Reset Validation Checksum byte.
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] • `$E000`: Applesoft BASIC Cold Start.
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] • `$E003`: Applesoft BASIC Warm Start (retains the program in memory).
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] How the Soft Reset Sequence Works
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] When a reset is triggered, the CPU fetches the address from `FFFC-FFFD` and execution starts at
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] `$FA62` in the ROM Monitor. The Monitor determines whether to perform a Soft Reset (Warm
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] Start) or a Hard Reset (Cold Boot) using the following validation check:
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] 1. The Monitor calculates an expected validation byte:
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash]    \textExpected Value = \textRAM[\$03F3] \oplus \$A5
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] 2. It compares this calculated value with the byte stored at `RAM[$03F4]`.
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] 3. If `RAM[03F4]` matches: The reset vector is valid. The Monitor executes an indirect jump
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] to the address stored in `03F2-03F3` (`JMP (03F2)`).
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] 4. If `RAM[03F4]` does not match: The vector is invalid or corrupted (e.g., after
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] power-up). The Monitor initializes system hardware, sets up standard vectors, and performs a cold
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] boot (usually booting disk or starting Applesoft Cold Start at `E000`).
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] When DOS, ProDOS, or Applesoft BASIC is running normally:
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] • `03F2-03F3` is typically set to point to the DOS/ProDOS re-entry vector or Applesoft Warm Start
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] (`$E003`).
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] • `03F4` is set to `HighByte(03F3) ^ $A5` to validate the soft reset vector.
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] Implementation Strategies in Your Emulator
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] Depending on how low-level your CPU and system emulation is, you can choose one of two approaches:
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] Approach 1: Low-Level / Accurate Emulation (Recommended)
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] Trigger a hardware reset on your emulated 6502 CPU.
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] 1. Assert the CPU Reset line or set the CPU Program Counter (`PC`) to the 16-bit address stored at
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] vector `FFFC` (typically `FA62`).
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] 2. Clear the interrupt disable flag if necessary, reset stack pointer logic as the 6502 hardware
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] does, and resume execution.
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] 3. Let the original ROM execution at `FA62` evaluate `03F2–$03F4` and jump to the warm start vector
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] automatically.
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] This ensures full fidelity with native hardware behavior and handles DOS, ProDOS, and custom
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] software hooks without special-casing.
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] Approach 2: High-Level Vector Override (Fast / Shortcut)
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] If you wish to bypass running the Monitor reset code directly:
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] 1. Read `RAM[03F3]` and `RAM[03F4]`.
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] 2. Compute `val = RAM[$03F3] ^ 0xA5`.
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] 3. If `val == RAM[$03F4]`:
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash]    * Set CPU `PC = RAM[03F2] | (RAM[03F3] << 8)`
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] 4. Else (invalid checksum):
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash]    * Set CPU `PC = 0xE000` (Applesoft Cold Start) or trigger a disk boot.
2026-07-22 19:25:52 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-07-22 19:31:50 [simon@midnightblue.local] what is prodos?
2026-07-22 19:31:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 1.9.9 gemini-3.6-flash 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.011 3.796KB 8.61s
2026-07-22 19:31:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] • ProDOS (Professional Disk Operating System) is the operating system introduced by Apple
2026-07-22 19:31:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] Computer in 1983 for the Apple II family of computers. It was designed to replace the aging DOS
2026-07-22 19:31:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] 3.3*, providing a more modern, faster, and expandable system environment.
2026-07-22 19:31:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-07-22 19:31:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-07-22 19:31:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-07-22 19:31:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] Key Features of ProDOS
2026-07-22 19:31:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-07-22 19:31:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] 1. Hierarchical Directory Structure
2026-07-22 19:31:59 [gemini-3.6-flash]    * Unlike DOS 3.3, which used a flat file system (all files stored in a single list on the disk),
2026-07-22 19:31:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] ProDOS introduced nested directories and subdirectories, similar to modern file systems (MS-DOS,
2026-07-22 19:31:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] Unix, macOS).
2026-07-22 19:31:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-07-22 19:31:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] 2. Support for Larger Storage Media
2026-07-22 19:31:59 [gemini-3.6-flash]    * DOS 3.3 was strictly tailored to 140 KB 5.25-inch floppy disks (Disk II).
2026-07-22 19:31:59 [gemini-3.6-flash]    * ProDOS natively supported larger media, including 3.5-inch floppies (800 KB), hard drives, and
2026-07-22 19:31:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] RAM disk expansions, with volume sizes up to 32 Megabytes.
2026-07-22 19:31:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-07-22 19:31:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] 3. Standardized API: The Machine Language Interface (MLI)
2026-07-22 19:31:59 [gemini-3.6-flash]    * ProDOS provided a uniform entry point (`JSR $BF00`) for software to perform disk I/O, file
2026-07-22 19:31:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] operations, and memory management. This eliminated the need for developers to bypass the OS or
2026-07-22 19:31:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] address hardware directly.
2026-07-22 19:31:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-07-22 19:31:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] 4. Improved Performance
2026-07-22 19:31:59 [gemini-3.6-flash]    * Disk read and write operations under ProDOS were significantly faster than under DOS 3.3 due
2026-07-22 19:31:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] to optimized block-based transfer routines.
2026-07-22 19:31:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-07-22 19:31:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] 5. Interoperability with BASIC
2026-07-22 19:31:59 [gemini-3.6-flash]    * ProDOS separated the core operating system kernel from the user environment. To run Applesoft
2026-07-22 19:31:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] BASIC programs, ProDOS loaded a system program named `BASIC.SYSTEM`, which translated traditional
2026-07-22 19:31:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] Applesoft disk commands (such as `CATALOG`, `BLOAD`, and `RUN`) into ProDOS MLI calls.
2026-07-22 19:31:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-07-22 19:31:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-07-22 19:31:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-07-22 19:31:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] ProDOS Versions
2026-07-22 19:31:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-07-22 19:31:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] • ProDOS 8: Designed for 8-bit Apple II computers (Apple II Plus with 64K, Apple IIe, IIc,
2026-07-22 19:31:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] and IIc Plus).
2026-07-22 19:31:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] • ProDOS 16 / GS/OS: Designed for the 16-bit Apple IIGS, providing full support for the
2026-07-22 19:31:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] 65816 processor and advanced system architecture.
2026-07-22 19:31:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-07-22 19:31:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-07-22 19:31:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-07-22 19:31:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] Relevance to Emulator Development
2026-07-22 19:31:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-07-22 19:31:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] When emulating an Apple II system running ProDOS:
2026-07-22 19:31:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] • Soft Reset Handling: ProDOS hooks the Soft Reset vector (`03F2–03F4`) during boot. When a
2026-07-22 19:31:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] soft reset occurs, execution vectors back into the ProDOS system handler to restore the command
2026-07-22 19:31:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] interpreter or System Selector safely.
2026-07-22 19:31:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] • Storage Emulation: Implementing block-level storage devices (such as SmartPort or ProDOS
2026-07-22 19:31:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] hard disk drives) allows your emulator to boot ProDOS directly from virtual hard drive images
2026-07-22 19:31:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] (`.2MG`, `.HDV`, or `.PO` format).
2026-07-22 19:31:59 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
