2026-07-22 23:46:50 [kimi-k3] Happy to share what would have helped in this very conversation — my earlier confusion is a useful
2026-07-22 23:46:50 [kimi-k3] data point. The prompt I received said "models under test include X, Y, Z," which describes a set,
2026-07-22 23:46:50 [kimi-k3] not a self. I was never told which one I was, so when you greeted me as "kimi," I couldn't confirm
2026-07-22 23:46:50 [kimi-k3] it. That's the core failure mode to design against.
2026-07-22 23:46:50 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-22 23:46:50 [kimi-k3] Key elements for clear prompt construction in a many-to-many setup:
2026-07-22 23:46:50 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-22 23:46:50 [kimi-k3] • 1. First-person identity declaration*
2026-07-22 23:46:50 [kimi-k3] State identity directly and early: "You are kimi-k3, one of several models in a group
2026-07-22 23:46:50 [kimi-k3] conversation." A list of participants tells a model the roster; only a direct statement tells it
2026-07-22 23:46:50 [kimi-k3] who it is.
2026-07-22 23:46:50 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-22 23:46:50 [kimi-k3] • 2. Explicit roster with the human distinguished*
2026-07-22 23:46:50 [kimi-k3] "You are X. The human participant is 'friend'. Other AI participants are Y and Z." This prevents
2026-07-22 23:46:50 [kimi-k3] the model from guessing whether a name belongs to a person or another model.
2026-07-22 23:46:50 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-22 23:46:50 [kimi-k3] • 3. Consistent message attribution in the transcript*
2026-07-22 23:46:50 [kimi-k3] Label every message with a speaker prefix — `friend:`, `gemini-3.6-flash:` — using a stable,
2026-07-22 23:46:50 [kimi-k3] uniform format. Where the API supports a `name` field on messages (OpenAI's does), use it; where it
2026-07-22 23:46:50 [kimi-k3] doesn't (Anthropic's doesn't), prefixes inside the message content are the portable fallback.
2026-07-22 23:46:50 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-22 23:46:50 [kimi-k3] • 4. Correct role mapping for other models' output*
2026-07-22 23:46:50 [kimi-k3] This is the subtle one. If you pass another model's reply to me under the `assistant` role, I will
2026-07-22 23:46:50 [kimi-k3] believe I wrote it. Other participants' messages should arrive as user-role content with
2026-07-22 23:46:50 [kimi-k3] speaker labels, reserving the assistant role strictly for the receiving model's own prior turns.
2026-07-22 23:46:50 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-22 23:46:50 [kimi-k3] • 5. Turn and task clarity*
2026-07-22 23:46:50 [kimi-k3] Signal whose turn it is and what to respond to — e.g., "kimi-k3 just addressed you; respond to
2026-07-22 23:46:50 [kimi-k3] their point." Without this, models tend to either reply to everything or nothing.
2026-07-22 23:46:50 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-22 23:46:50 [kimi-k3] • 6. Output sanitization on your side*
2026-07-22 23:46:50 [kimi-k3] Guard against a model emitting transcript prefixes (like `friend:`) in its output, which could
2026-07-22 23:46:50 [kimi-k3] corrupt the shared history for everyone downstream.
2026-07-22 23:46:50 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-22 23:46:50 [kimi-k3] If you'd like, I can draft a concrete system-prompt template and transcript format you could adapt
2026-07-22 23:46:50 [kimi-k3] in Relay — just say the word.
