2026-07-24 17:04:18 [simon@midnightblue.local] oh, that is helpful, i did not pick up that there was an Apple I integer basic
2026-07-24 17:04:36 [kimi-k3] 🟠nitrologic Relay 1.9.9 kimi-k3 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.012 11.51KB 18.19s
2026-07-24 17:04:36 [kimi-k3] Yes—there was an Apple I Integer BASIC.
2026-07-24 17:04:36 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 17:04:36 [kimi-k3] The key distinction is:
2026-07-24 17:04:36 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 17:04:36 [kimi-k3] - Apple I Integer BASIC
2026-07-24 17:04:36 [kimi-k3]   - Written by Steve Wozniak.
2026-07-24 17:04:36 [kimi-k3]   - Very small, tape-oriented BASIC.
2026-07-24 17:04:36 [kimi-k3]   - No Apple II-specific graphics commands like `GR`, `COLOR`, `PLOT`.
2026-07-24 17:04:36 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 17:04:36 [kimi-k3] - Apple II Integer BASIC
2026-07-24 17:04:36 [kimi-k3]   - Descended from / adapted from the Apple I version.
2026-07-24 17:04:36 [kimi-k3]   - Added support for Apple II features, especially low-resolution graphics and related I/O.
2026-07-24 17:04:37 [kimi-k3]   - This is why Brick Out / Little Brick Out could be Integer BASIC on the Apple II.
2026-07-24 17:04:37 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 17:04:37 [kimi-k3] So if someone says “Integer BASIC,” the right follow-up is often: “Apple I or Apple II?”
2026-07-24 17:04:37 [kimi-k3] The command set differs enough that answers can otherwise sound contradictory.
2026-07-24 17:04:37 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 17:04:37 [kimi-k3] A cautious rule of thumb:
2026-07-24 17:04:37 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 17:04:37 [kimi-k3] - Core integer-only language: shared heritage
2026-07-24 17:04:37 [kimi-k3] - Apple II extensions: graphics and machine-specific features
2026-07-24 17:04:37 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 17:04:37 [kimi-k3] So your earlier markdown makes much more sense if it is specifically for Apple II Integer
2026-07-24 17:04:37 [kimi-k3] BASIC, not the Apple I version.
2026-07-24 17:04:37 [kimi-k3] 
