2026-07-24 17:08:23 [kimi-k3] 🟠nitrologic Relay 1.9.9 kimi-k3 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.024 15.67KB 30.16s
2026-07-24 17:08:23 [kimi-k3] Those three are in both — `NORMAL`, `FLASH`, and `INVERSE` are part of Apple II Integer
2026-07-24 17:08:23 [kimi-k3] BASIC, not exclusive to Applesoft.
2026-07-24 17:08:23 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 17:08:23 [kimi-k3] They control how subsequent `PRINT` output appears:
2026-07-24 17:08:23 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 17:08:23 [kimi-k3] - NORMAL – Standard white-on-black text.
2026-07-24 17:08:23 [kimi-k3] - INVERSE – Reversed video (black on white).
2026-07-24 17:08:23 [kimi-k3] - FLASH – Flashing text.
2026-07-24 17:08:23 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 17:08:23 [kimi-k3] Integer BASIC on the Apple II also has other text-screen commands you might want for your document,
2026-07-24 17:08:23 [kimi-k3] such as:
2026-07-24 17:08:23 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 17:08:23 [kimi-k3] - HOME – Clear the screen and move the cursor to the top-left.
2026-07-24 17:08:23 [kimi-k3] - TAB – Move to a column (as a statement).
2026-07-24 17:08:23 [kimi-k3] - VTAB / HTAB – Position the cursor vertically / horizontally.
2026-07-24 17:08:23 [kimi-k3] - SPEED – Control character output rate.
2026-07-24 17:08:23 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 17:08:23 [kimi-k3] So a fair acknowledgment: my earlier "24 commands" tally for Apple II Integer BASIC was still
2026-07-24 17:08:23 [kimi-k3] incomplete — the full Apple II keyword set also includes this text-display group. If you're keeping
2026-07-24 17:08:23 [kimi-k3] the markdown as a curated learning list rather than a complete reference, that's fine, but a "Text
2026-07-24 17:08:23 [kimi-k3] Display" section with `NORMAL`, `INVERSE`, `FLASH`, and `HOME` would fit naturally alongside your
2026-07-24 17:08:23 [kimi-k3] Graphics section.
