2026-07-24 19:46:53 [kimi-k3] • 40 × 48* — 40 columns wide, 48 rows tall.
2026-07-24 19:46:53 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 19:46:53 [kimi-k3] The wrinkle that trips people up: the low-res mode shares the text screen's 40×24 character memory.
2026-07-24 19:46:53 [kimi-k3] Each character cell is split into two vertically stacked blocks (one byte = two nybbles,
2026-07-24 19:46:53 [kimi-k3] upper nybble is the bottom block, lower nybble is the top block), which is how 24 text rows become
2026-07-24 19:46:53 [kimi-k3] 48 graphics rows.
2026-07-24 19:46:53 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 19:46:53 [kimi-k3] Two consequences worth noting for your emulator:
2026-07-24 19:46:53 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 19:46:53 [kimi-k3] 1. GR leaves 4 lines of text. When you enter `GR` mode, only the top 40 rows are graphics —
2026-07-24 19:46:53 [kimi-k3] the bottom 8 rows (4 text lines) remain visible as a text window for `PRINT` output. Full 40×48
2026-07-24 19:46:53 [kimi-k3] graphics requires poking the soft switch for mixed-mode off (`POKE 49234,0` or equivalent), which
2026-07-24 19:46:53 [kimi-k3] Integer BASIC has no keyword for.
2026-07-24 19:46:53 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 19:46:53 [kimi-k3] 2. Coordinates are y-major in PLOT. `PLOT x, y` takes x = 0–39 across, y = 0–47 down from
2026-07-24 19:46:53 [kimi-k3] the top. `HLIN` and `VLIN` follow from that grid.
2026-07-24 19:46:53 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 19:46:53 [kimi-k3] The 16 colours (`COLOR=0` through `15`) map to the standard Apple II palette: 0 black, 1 magenta, 2
2026-07-24 19:46:53 [kimi-k3] dark blue, 3 purple, 4 dark green, 5 grey, 6 medium blue, 7 light blue, 8 brown, 9 orange, 10 grey,
2026-07-24 19:46:53 [kimi-k3] 11 pink, 12 green, 13 yellow, 14 aqua, 15 white. I'd verify that mapping against an emulator
2026-07-24 19:46:53 [kimi-k3] screenshot rather than trusting my ordering of the middle entries, but 0=black and 15=white are
2026-07-24 19:46:53 [kimi-k3] certain anchors.
2026-07-24 19:46:53 [kimi-k3] 
