2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] Two files this time — and `machine.ts` answers a lot of questions about the project. Comments on
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] each.
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] integerbasic.md
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] The five stragglers (`POP`, `TRACE`, `NOTRACE`, `PR#`, `IN#`) are appended at the bottom without a
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] home. Suggested placement:
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] - POP → Program Flow. It discards the most recent `GOSUB` return address, so it's stack
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] manipulation — same family as `GOSUB`/`RETURN`.
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] - TRACE / NOTRACE → a small "Debugging" section, or fold into Immediate Mode. Worth noting
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] these are Integer BASIC only — Applesoft dropped them, so they're a nice fingerprint of the
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] dialect.
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] - PR# / IN# → Fundamentals or their own "Device Redirection" note. They reroute
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] output/input to a slot (e.g., `PR#1` for a printer card). On a bare machine they're mostly no-ops
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] or hangs.
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] machine.ts
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] This is the first look at the actual emulator — a Deno-based 6502 interpreter covering the Atari
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] vector titles (Lunar Lander, Asteroids, Centipede, Battlezone via the DVG) and now Apple II/II+.
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] Impressively compact for what it does. A few things that matter for the Integer BASIC goal:
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] • 1. Soft-switch writes will halt the machine (the blocker for GR).*
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] In `writeAddress`, any write to `C001`–`C0FF` logs and sets `stepping=false`. But Apple II soft
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] switches respond to any access, read or write — and Integer BASIC's `GR` statement
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] performs `STA C050`. So the first graphics command in Brick Out will stop the emulator. The fix is
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] to route writes to `C050`–`$C05F` through the same handler as reads (even if the handler is
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] currently stubbed), rather than treating them as errors.
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] • 2. `$C052` is missing from the read switch.*
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] Mixed-mode-off (the full 40×48 screen, no 4-line text window) is accessed at `C052` — Brick Out-era
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] programs commonly touch it. Right now a read falls through to the ` C0XX` halt. Add it
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] alongside `C050`/`C051`.
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] • 3. No lores renderer yet.*
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] `displayApple` renders the text screen only. Lores is the same `400`–`7FF` memory,
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] reinterpreted: each byte is two stacked blocks, low nybble on top, high nybble on bottom, 40×48.
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] Since you're already in a terminal, upper/lower half-block characters (`▀` with
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] foreground/background colours) would map onto this naturally — one terminal row per byte-row,
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] 16-colour ANSI foreground/background per nybble.
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] • 4. ROM selection: you want `apple2`, not `apple2plus`, for Integer BASIC.*
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] The II Plus autostart ROM boots into Applesoft. Your `roms` array currently selects `apple2plus`;
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] `apple2` is the Integer BASIC machine. You have both imported already, so it's a one-line flip —
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] and a nice A/B test since Applesoft boots differently (its `]` prompt vs Integer BASIC's `>`
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] prompt).
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] • 5. Minor: IRQ vectors to the Reset address.*
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] In `interrupt()`, the non-NMI path loads the vector from `FFFC` (Reset), but IRQ should use `FFFE`.
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] Also, the 6502 sets the I flag on IRQ entry, not just NMI. This is currently dormant (the IRQ path
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] is commented out), so it's a note for later rather than a live bug.
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] • 6. ROM write-protection.*
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] Memory is flat writable RAM, including the burned ROM regions. Fine for now, but a stray Integer
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] BASIC `POKE` into `$E000`+ will silently corrupt the interpreter. A range check in `writeAddress`
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] would turn those into log events instead of confusing behaviour.
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] • 7. Flash/inverse logic in `displayApple` looks right.*
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] The `0x40`–`0x7F` flash window gated on `frameCount & 64` matches the original Apple II charset
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] behaviour.
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] The cassette WAV round-trip (record cycle deltas, emit 22050 Hz WAV, re-ingest at startup) is a
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] tidy design — and once `LOAD`/`SAVE` work, that's the path to getting period tape images of Integer
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] BASIC programs into the machine without typing them.
2026-07-24 19:50:32 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 19:52:52 [simon@midnightblue.local] currently with the older rom, it boots into the rom and then i need to use ctrl b enter to run integer basic, does that sound correct?
2026-07-24 19:53:21 [kimi-k3] 🟠nitrologic Relay 1.9.9 kimi-k3 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.120 131.0KB 28.16s
2026-07-24 19:53:21 [kimi-k3] Yes, that's exactly right — and it's good news for your emulation accuracy.
2026-07-24 19:53:21 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 19:53:21 [kimi-k3] The original Apple II (non-Plus, 1977) shipped with the "Monitor ROM" (also called the "old
2026-07-24 19:53:21 [kimi-k3] monitor" or "System Monitor"). On power-up/reset it drops you into the Monitor at the `*` prompt.
2026-07-24 19:53:21 [kimi-k3] From there:
2026-07-24 19:53:21 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 19:53:21 [kimi-k3] - Ctrl-B (then Enter) → enters Integer BASIC, giving you the `>` prompt.
2026-07-24 19:53:21 [kimi-k3] - Ctrl-C → re-enter the currently resident BASIC (warm start).
2026-07-24 19:53:21 [kimi-k3] - Monitor commands like `E000G` would also work, since Integer BASIC lives at `$E000`.
2026-07-24 19:53:21 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 19:53:21 [kimi-k3] The behaviour you're describing — boot to `*`, then Ctrl-B, Enter, then `>` — is the authentic
2026-07-24 19:53:21 [kimi-k3] power-on experience of a 1977 Apple II with Integer BASIC in ROM. It was the Apple II Plus
2026-07-24 19:53:21 [kimi-k3] (1978) whose Autostart ROM skipped the Monitor and booted straight into Applesoft (or ran a
2026-07-24 19:53:21 [kimi-k3] disk if present).
2026-07-24 19:53:21 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 19:53:21 [kimi-k3] A couple of details worth knowing for your emulator:
2026-07-24 19:53:21 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 19:53:21 [kimi-k3] 1. Ctrl-B is just a Monitor command. The Monitor scans the input line for control commands;
2026-07-24 19:53:21 [kimi-k3] `B` with the high bit set (`0x82`) triggers the "enter BASIC" routine. So your keyboard path
2026-07-24 19:53:22 [kimi-k3] through `C000`/`C010` needs to deliver it like any other keypress — which, from your
2026-07-24 19:53:22 [kimi-k3] `pushKey`/`keyBuffer` code, it looks like you're set up to do.
2026-07-24 19:53:22 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 19:53:22 [kimi-k3] 2. The reset vector flow. On reset, the old Monitor does its init, rings the bell, and
2026-07-24 19:53:22 [kimi-k3] waits at `*`. If your `machine.ts` is reaching that point from the `apple2` ROM with just the
2026-07-24 19:53:22 [kimi-k3] opcodes you've implemented so far, your 6502 coverage is already in decent shape — the Monitor
2026-07-24 19:53:22 [kimi-k3] exercises a fair amount of the instruction set.
2026-07-24 19:53:22 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 19:53:22 [kimi-k3] 3. Handy test milestone: once Ctrl-B gets you the `>` prompt, the canonical smoke test is:
2026-07-24 19:53:22 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 19:53:22 [kimi-k3]    >PRINT 3/2
2026-07-24 19:53:22 [kimi-k3]    1
2026-07-24 19:53:22 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 19:53:22 [kimi-k3]    If it prints `1` (integer truncation) rather than `1.5`, you're in genuine Integer BASIC, not
2026-07-24 19:53:22 [kimi-k3] accidentally running Applesoft.
2026-07-24 19:53:22 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 19:53:22 [kimi-k3] So: correct behaviour, keep it. If you later want the "plug in and it's already in BASIC"
2026-07-24 19:53:22 [kimi-k3] experience, that would be historically wrong for this ROM — the II Plus / Autostart behaviour
2026-07-24 19:53:22 [kimi-k3] belongs to `apple2plus` only.
2026-07-24 19:53:22 [kimi-k3] 
