2026-07-24 20:02:57 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:02:57 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:02:57 [kimi-k3] machine.ts
2026-07-24 20:02:57 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:02:57 [kimi-k3] • The graphics path is stubbed but not wired up.* `displayAppleGraphics` and
2026-07-24 20:02:57 [kimi-k3] `initAppleBlocks` are new, and the structure is right — a precomputed 256-entry lookup indexed by
2026-07-24 20:02:57 [kimi-k3] byte, one `▀`-style entry per nybble pair. But:
2026-07-24 20:02:57 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:02:57 [kimi-k3] 1. `initAppleBlocks()` is never called, and the loop body assigns an empty `block` string
2026-07-24 20:02:57 [kimi-k3] (and a `""` that TS will flag as missing a semicolon-tolerated expression — `const block=""` with
2026-07-24 20:02:57 [kimi-k3] no semicolon then `appleBlock[i]=block` works, but every entry will be blank).
2026-07-24 20:02:57 [kimi-k3] 2. `displayAppleGraphics` is never called either — `tick()` still only calls `displayApple`
2026-07-24 20:02:57 [kimi-k3] in the vblank path.
2026-07-24 20:02:57 [kimi-k3] 3. No state tracking for the soft switches. Reads of `C050`/`C051`/`C052`/`C056` currently
2026-07-24 20:02:57 [kimi-k3] `return 0` without recording anything. You need flags like `gfxTextMode`, `gfxMixed`, `gfxLores`,
2026-07-24 20:02:57 [kimi-k3] set in both `readAddress` and `writeAddress` (this is the part from last time that still
2026-07-24 20:02:57 [kimi-k3] isn't fixed — writes to `C0xx` still halt the machine, and Integer BASIC's `GR` does `STA
2026-07-24 20:02:57 [kimi-k3] C050`). Then `tick()` picks `displayApple` vs `displayAppleGraphics` based on those flags.
2026-07-24 20:02:57 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:02:57 [kimi-k3] Suggested shape for the block table, once you wire it up:
2026-07-24 20:02:57 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:02:57 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:02:57 [kimi-k3] ▀ with fg = top-nybble colour, bg = bottom-nybble colour
2026-07-24 20:02:57 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:02:57 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:02:57 [kimi-k3] ANSI 256-colour or truecolour escapes let you map the 16 Apple II colours onto
2026-07-24 20:02:57 [kimi-k3] foreground/background pairs. Since there are only 256 byte values, precomputing full escape strings
2026-07-24 20:02:57 [kimi-k3] at init (as you're doing) is exactly right for per-frame rendering speed.
2026-07-24 20:02:57 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:02:57 [kimi-k3] • Small correctness notes on the lores layout* in `displayAppleGraphics`:
2026-07-24 20:02:57 [kimi-k3] - The row-to-address math (`0x400 + 40(y>>3) + 128(y&7)`) is the text-screen mapping, which
2026-07-24 20:02:57 [kimi-k3] is correct — lores shares the same screen holes. Good.
2026-07-24 20:02:57 [kimi-k3] - Each of the 24 rows renders as two block-rows, so the graphics display will be 48 "lines" worth
2026-07-24 20:02:57 [kimi-k3] of content — your terminal will need 48 rows for full-screen mode, or you render 20 rows of
2026-07-24 20:02:57 [kimi-k3] graphics + 4 rows of text in mixed mode. The mixed-mode split (graphics rows 0–39 = text rows 0–19)
2026-07-24 20:02:57 [kimi-k3] is where the real Apple II GR behaviour lives.
2026-07-24 20:02:57 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:02:57 [kimi-k3] • The `interrupt()` IRQ vector* (from last review) is unchanged — still uses `$FFFC` for
2026-07-24 20:02:57 [kimi-k3] the non-NMI case. Dormant for now, but worth a `// TODO` since Reset and IRQ sharing a vector will
2026-07-24 20:02:57 [kimi-k3] bite when you enable vblank IRQs.
2026-07-24 20:02:57 [kimi-k3] 
