2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] 🟠nitrologic Relay 1.9.9 kimi-k3 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.213 229.5KB 33.77s
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] Good question — the standard spelling is nibble (with an i). "Nyble" or "nybble"
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] with a y shows up occasionally in older hacker writing (Jargon File-era playfulness, same
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] instinct that gave us "byte" instead of "bite"), but the mainstream spelling across datasheets,
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] textbooks, and modern code is nibble. So: `hiNibble`/`loNibble` if you're naming variables.
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] For the lookup table, the pieces you need:
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] • The 16 Apple II lores colours*, as ANSI truecolour approximations. The canonical RGB
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] values (widely used by AppleWin and others):
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] const appleColors = [
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] 	[0, 0, 0],       // 0  black
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] 	[227, 30, 96],   // 1  magenta
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] 	[96, 78, 189],   // 2  dark blue
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] 	[255, 68, 253],  // 3  purple
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] 	[0, 163, 96],    // 4  dark green
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] 	[156, 156, 156], // 5  grey
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] 	[20, 207, 253],  // 6  medium blue
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] 	[208, 195, 255], // 7  light blue
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] 	[96, 114, 3],    // 8  brown
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] 	[255, 106, 60],  // 9  orange
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] 	[156, 156, 156], // 10 grey
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] 	[255, 160, 208], // 11 pink
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] 	[20, 245, 60],   // 12 green
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] 	[208, 221, 141], // 13 yellow
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] 	[114, 255, 208], // 14 aqua
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] 	[255, 255, 255], // 15 white
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] ];
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] • The block construction. Each byte is two vertically stacked blocks: low nibble on
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] top (y even), high nibble on bottom* (y odd). One `▀` character carries both:
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] foreground = top, background = bottom.
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] function initAppleBlocks() {
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] 	for (let i = 0; i < 256; i++) {
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] 		const top = i & 15;      // low nibble = upper block
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] 		const bottom = i >> 4;   // high nibble = lower block
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] 		const [tr, tg, tb] = appleColors[top];
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] 		const [br, bg, bb] = appleColors[bottom];
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] 		appleBlock[i] = `\x1b[38;2;${tr};${tg};${tb}m\x1b[48;2;${br};${bg};${bb}m▀`;
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] 	}
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] }
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] The trailing `cursorRestore` in `displayAppleGraphics` then resets attributes once per frame rather
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] than per cell, keeping the escape noise down.
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] • One design note:* truecolour escape sequences are long (~40 chars per cell × 40 columns ×
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] 24 rows ≈ 38 KB per frame). If the terminal flickers, the fallback is the 216-colour cube of ANSI
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] 256-colour mode (`\x1b[38;5;Nm`), which is much shorter and close enough for these 16 saturated
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] colours.
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] Also worth remembering: since the two nibbles of a byte frequently match (solid rows of colour,
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] e.g. `COLOR=1; HLIN 0,39 AT 20` writes bytes like `0x11`), a large fraction of your cells will be
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] fg == bg, where `█` with only a foreground escape would be shorter — but that's an optimisation for
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] later. Correctness first.
2026-07-24 20:06:35 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:07:27 [simon@midnightblue.local] i was hoping to avoid true color, will a simpler ansi color mode not work?
2026-07-24 20:08:09 [kimi-k3] 🟠nitrologic Relay 1.9.9 kimi-k3 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.216 232.3KB 41.31s
2026-07-24 20:08:09 [kimi-k3] It'll work, yes — with caveats. The standard 16 ANSI colours (30–37 fg / 40–47 bg, plus 90–97 /
2026-07-24 20:08:09 [kimi-k3] 100–107 brights) give you exactly 16 slots, which is a pleasing match for the Apple II's 16 lores
2026-07-24 20:08:09 [kimi-k3] colours. The problem is mapping: the Apple II palette doesn't line up with ANSI's palette.
2026-07-24 20:08:09 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:08:09 [kimi-k3] Roughly:
2026-07-24 20:08:09 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:08:09 [kimi-k3] ╭──────────┬───────────────┬─────────────────────┬──────────────────────╮
2026-07-24 20:08:09 [kimi-k3] │  COLOR=  │  Apple II     │  Nearest ANSI       │  Quality             │
2026-07-24 20:08:09 [kimi-k3] ├──────────┼───────────────┼─────────────────────┼──────────────────────┤
2026-07-24 20:08:09 [kimi-k3] │  0       │  black        │  30 black           │  exact               │
2026-07-24 20:08:09 [kimi-k3] │  1       │  magenta      │  91 bright red      │  decent              │
2026-07-24 20:08:09 [kimi-k3] │  2       │  dark blue    │  34 blue            │  decent              │
2026-07-24 20:08:09 [kimi-k3] │  3       │  purple       │  95 bright magenta  │  decent              │
2026-07-24 20:08:09 [kimi-k3] │  4       │  dark green   │  32 green           │  decent              │
2026-07-24 20:08:09 [kimi-k3] │  5       │  grey         │  90 bright black    │  decent              │
