2026-07-24 20:19:54 [kimi-k3] Yes — in mixed mode the bottom 4 lines are real text, rendered through the same character generator
2026-07-24 20:19:54 [kimi-k3] as text mode, so `FLASH` (and `INVERSE`) apply there exactly as they do in full text mode.
2026-07-24 20:19:54 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:19:54 [kimi-k3] Concretely for your renderer: mixed mode means rows 20–23 of the text screen layout need
2026-07-24 20:19:54 [kimi-k3] the full `displayApple` treatment — the inverse/flash logic, the `frameCount & 64` blink gating,
2026-07-24 20:19:54 [kimi-k3] all of it. So the cleanest structure is to not duplicate that code: give `displayApple` and
2026-07-24 20:19:54 [kimi-k3] `displayAppleGraphics` a row-range parameter, or factor a `renderTextRow(memory, y, frameCount)`
2026-07-24 20:19:54 [kimi-k3] helper used by both paths. Something like:
2026-07-24 20:19:54 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:19:54 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:19:54 [kimi-k3] lores-mixed:
2026-07-24 20:19:54 [kimi-k3]   rows 0–19  → graphics (nibble blocks)
2026-07-24 20:19:54 [kimi-k3]   rows 20–23 → text (with flash/inverse)
2026-07-24 20:19:54 [kimi-k3] text mode:
2026-07-24 20:19:54 [kimi-k3]   rows 0–23  → text (with flash/inverse)
2026-07-24 20:19:54 [kimi-k3] lores-full ($C052):
2026-07-24 20:19:54 [kimi-k3]   rows 0–23  → graphics
2026-07-24 20:19:54 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:19:54 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:19:54 [kimi-k3] Note the subtlety on where the split lands: the text window occupies the last four rows of the text
2026-07-24 20:19:54 [kimi-k3] memory map (the `7D0`–`7FF` region… well, the rows whose base addresses land there), and your
2026-07-24 20:19:54 [kimi-k3] existing `offset` formula already computes the right addresses for those rows — so the graphics
2026-07-24 20:19:54 [kimi-k3] path just needs to stop at y=20 in mixed mode, and the text path picks up from y=20.
2026-07-24 20:19:54 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:19:54 [kimi-k3] The flash timing detail carries over unchanged: `frameCount & 64` gives you roughly a half-second
2026-07-24 20:19:54 [kimi-k3] blink cycle at 60 fps, which is in the right ballpark for the real hardware's ~2 Hz flash rate. One
2026-07-24 20:19:54 [kimi-k3] caveat — the Apple II's actual flash rate is closer to 1.875 Hz (a full on+off cycle about every 32
2026-07-24 20:19:54 [kimi-k3] frames), so `frameCount & 16` would be more faithful if you care; `& 64` flashes a bit lazily.
2026-07-24 20:19:54 [kimi-k3] Cosmetic either way.
2026-07-24 20:19:54 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:19:54 [kimi-k3] And yes — `frameCount` in `displayAppleGraphics` stops being unused the moment mixed mode lands,
2026-07-24 20:19:54 [kimi-k3] since it has to pass through to the text rows. So keeping the parameter was the right call after
2026-07-24 20:19:54 [kimi-k3] all.
