2026-07-24 20:46:34 [simon@midnightblue.local] cursorRestore is not resetting the bg fg colors, is there an ansi code ?
2026-07-24 20:47:01 [kimi-k3] 🟠nitrologic Relay 1.9.9 kimi-k3 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.386 427.5KB 25.50s
2026-07-24 20:47:01 [kimi-k3] `\x1b[0m` should reset foreground and background — it's SGR reset, which clears all
2026-07-24 20:47:01 [kimi-k3] attributes including colours. So if your `cursorRestore` (`\x1b[0m`) isn't restoring colours, a few
2026-07-24 20:47:01 [kimi-k3] possibilities:
2026-07-24 20:47:01 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:47:01 [kimi-k3] 1. It's not being emitted where you think. In the new unified `displayApple`, I notice the
2026-07-24 20:47:01 [kimi-k3] `console.log(cursorRestore)` at the end didn't survive the refactor — the old
2026-07-24 20:47:01 [kimi-k3] `displayAppleGraphics` had it, but the merged function doesn't. If nothing after the graphics block
2026-07-24 20:47:01 [kimi-k3] emits a reset, every cell's bg colour bleeds into the next line's default spaces, the scroll
2026-07-24 20:47:01 [kimi-k3] region, etc. Check whether that's the actual cause before suspecting the escape code.
2026-07-24 20:47:01 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:47:01 [kimi-k3] 2. If you want targeted resets rather than full SGR reset:
2026-07-24 20:47:01 [kimi-k3]    - `\x1b[39m` — default foreground
2026-07-24 20:47:01 [kimi-k3]    - `\x1b[49m` — default background
2026-07-24 20:47:01 [kimi-k3]    - `\x1b[39;49m` — both
2026-07-24 20:47:01 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:47:01 [kimi-k3]    Useful when you want to keep other attributes (bold, etc.) but drop colours.
2026-07-24 20:47:01 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:47:01 [kimi-k3] 3. Terminal quirks. Some terminals (tmux with certain `TERM` entries, older Windows
2026-07-24 20:47:01 [kimi-k3] consoles) have patchy `0m` handling. But macOS Terminal/iTerm2 handle it fine, so unlikely to be
2026-07-24 20:47:01 [kimi-k3] your issue.
2026-07-24 20:47:01 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:47:01 [kimi-k3] Also worth a habit: emit the reset per line at the end of each `line.join("")`, not just
2026-07-24 20:47:01 [kimi-k3] once per frame. Otherwise any trailing background colour paints the rest of the row to the right
2026-07-24 20:47:01 [kimi-k3] edge, and autowrap/newline inherits it. Per-frame reset alone leaves 24 rows' worth of right-edge
2026-07-24 20:47:01 [kimi-k3] smear. So:
2026-07-24 20:47:01 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:47:01 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:47:01 [kimi-k3] console.log(line.join("") + cursorRestore);
2026-07-24 20:47:01 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:47:01 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:47:01 [kimi-k3] …or define a `sgrReset` constant if you want to keep `cursorRestore` for shutdown use only.
2026-07-24 20:47:01 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:49:47 [kimi-k3] 🟠nitrologic Relay 1.9.9 kimi-k3 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.466 519.5KB 33.96s
2026-07-24 20:49:47 [kimi-k3] Nice — the soft switches now drive `displayMode` as a two-bit field, and `cursorRestore` sits
2026-07-24 20:49:47 [kimi-k3] between the graphics and text sections. The bit-flag approach (`C050` sets bit 1, `C052/53`
2026-07-24 20:49:47 [kimi-k3] sets/clears bit 0) is a neat compression of the state. A few things to check:
2026-07-24 20:49:47 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:49:47 [kimi-k3] • The enum now has a dummy entry. `DisplayMode{TEXT,TEXTTEXT,LORES,MIXED}` — because
2026-07-24 20:49:47 [kimi-k3] `TEXT` is 0 and you need `MIXED` to be 3 (bits 0+1), the values are 0,1,2,3 where `TEXTTEXT`=1
2026-07-24 20:49:47 [kimi-k3] occupies the "mixed bit set, graphics bit clear" combination. That's the undefined* hardware
2026-07-24 20:49:47 [kimi-k3] state ($C053 mixed touched while in text mode), so a placeholder is honest — though worth a
2026-07-24 20:49:47 [kimi-k3] comment, since `TEXTTEXT` reads like a typo to anyone else. Alternatively rename it something like
2026-07-24 20:49:47 [kimi-k3] `UNUSED1`.
2026-07-24 20:49:47 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:49:47 [kimi-k3] • Check the splitY table matches the enum values:*
2026-07-24 20:49:47 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:49:47 [kimi-k3] const splitY=[0,0,20,24][mode];
2026-07-24 20:49:47 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:49:47 [kimi-k3] - 0 TEXT → 0 graphics rows. Correct.
2026-07-24 20:49:47 [kimi-k3] - 1 TEXTTEXT → 0. Undefined state treated as text. Fine.
2026-07-24 20:49:47 [kimi-k3] - 2 LORES → 20. Wrong way round! LORES should be 24 graphics rows (full screen), MIXED
2026-07-24 20:49:47 [kimi-k3] should be 20.
2026-07-24 20:49:47 [kimi-k3] - 3 MIXED → 24. Should be 20.
2026-07-24 20:49:47 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:49:47 [kimi-k3] So the table should be `[0,0,24,20]`. As written, `GR` (which produces mode 3) renders full-screen
2026-07-24 20:49:47 [kimi-k3] graphics with no text window, and full-graphics mode 2 renders mixed. Easy transposition to make.
2026-07-24 20:49:47 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:49:47 [kimi-k3] • Writes to C0xx still halt. Third review noting this — the `writeAddress` guard is
2026-07-24 20:49:47 [kimi-k3] untouched. Integer BASIC's `GR` does `STA C050`, and on real hardware writes to soft
2026-07-24 20:49:47 [kimi-k3] switches work identically to reads. Right now the mode bits only change if the ROM reads* those
2026-07-24 20:49:48 [kimi-k3] addresses. Some ROM routines do use `BIT` (read) to set switches, which is why your read path may
2026-07-24 20:49:48 [kimi-k3] appear to work, but `STA`-based code will stop the machine. The switch cases in `readAddress` need
2026-07-24 20:49:48 [kimi-k3] to be callable from `writeAddress` too — extract a `softSwitch(address)` method used by both paths.
2026-07-24 20:49:48 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:49:48 [kimi-k3] • The cursorRestore placement* is now between the two sections — good, it prevents graphics
2026-07-24 20:49:48 [kimi-k3] colours bleeding into the text window. The earlier per-line suggestion still applies for the
2026-07-24 20:49:48 [kimi-k3] right-edge smear, but if the display looks clean in practice, don't chase it.
2026-07-24 20:49:48 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:49:48 [kimi-k3] • Minor:* `displayMode:number` on the class could be typed `DisplayMode` now that the enum
2026-07-24 20:49:48 [kimi-k3] exists. And the big comment block documenting the $C05x mapping above `readAddress` is useful —
2026-07-24 20:49:48 [kimi-k3] keep it, though it slightly duplicates the inline `// gfx:` comments.
