2026-07-24 20:52:00 [kimi-k3] 🟠nitrologic Relay 1.9.9 kimi-k3 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.472 522.1KB 35.15s
2026-07-24 20:52:00 [kimi-k3] You're not missing anything — the flow is exactly:
2026-07-24 20:52:00 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:52:00 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:52:00 [kimi-k3] GR
2026-07-24 20:52:00 [kimi-k3] COLOR=1
2026-07-24 20:52:00 [kimi-k3] PLOT 0,0
2026-07-24 20:52:00 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:52:00 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:52:00 [kimi-k3] `COLOR=` is an assignment statement, not a function call — you write `COLOR=1` (or
2026-07-24 20:52:00 [kimi-k3] `COLOR=15` etc.), with values 0–15 selecting from the lores palette. It sets the colour that
2026-07-24 20:52:00 [kimi-k3] subsequent `PLOT`, `HLIN`, and `VLIN` use.
2026-07-24 20:52:00 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:52:00 [kimi-k3] A couple of notes for your testing:
2026-07-24 20:52:00 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:52:00 [kimi-k3] - After `GR` alone, the drawing colour is whatever was last set — on a fresh boot, 0 (black). So
2026-07-24 20:52:00 [kimi-k3] `PLOT` right after `GR` draws black-on-black: it works, you just can't see it. If your
2026-07-24 20:52:00 [kimi-k3] emulator shows nothing after `GR` + `PLOT 0,0`, the first suspect is "you plotted black", the
2026-07-24 20:52:00 [kimi-k3] second is the mode bits.
2026-07-24 20:52:00 [kimi-k3] - `COLOR` persists across `PLOT`s, so `COLOR=1: PLOT 0,0: PLOT 1,1` plots two magenta blocks.
2026-07-24 20:52:00 [kimi-k3] - A good smoke-test line that exercises GR, COLOR, and HLIN in one go, typed at the `>` prompt:
2026-07-24 20:52:00 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:52:00 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:52:00 [kimi-k3] GR: COLOR=1: HLIN 0,39 AT 0: COLOR=12: HLIN 0,39 AT 1
2026-07-24 20:52:00 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:52:00 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:52:00 [kimi-k3] That should give you a magenta bar across the top and a green one just under it, with the text
2026-07-24 20:52:00 [kimi-k3] window still alive at the bottom (mode 3, once the splitY table is fixed).
2026-07-24 20:52:00 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:52:00 [kimi-k3] - And the inverse check: `SCRN(0,0)` in a `PRINT` should return the colour you plotted — `PRINT
2026-07-24 20:52:00 [kimi-k3] SCRN(0,0)` after the magenta plot should print `1`. That's a handy way to verify your nibble
2026-07-24 20:52:00 [kimi-k3] ordering (low nibble = top block) without eyeballing colours.
2026-07-24 20:52:00 [kimi-k3] 
