2026-07-24 20:56:25 [simon@midnightblue.local] how does hires work?
2026-07-24 20:57:02 [kimi-k3] 🟠nitrologic Relay 1.9.9 kimi-k3 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.484 527.7KB 36.03s
2026-07-24 20:57:02 [kimi-k3] Hires is a different beast entirely — different memory, different encoding, different everything.
2026-07-24 20:57:02 [kimi-k3] Here's the shape of it, and importantly, how much of it applies to your Integer BASIC goals:
2026-07-24 20:57:02 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:57:02 [kimi-k3] • Memory layout. Hires page 1 lives at `2000`–`3FFF` (8 KB), page 2 at `4000`–`5FFF`. It's
2026-07-24 20:57:02 [kimi-k3] a bit-mapped display: 280 × 192 pixels*, one bit per pixel, seven pixels per byte. The
2026-07-24 20:57:02 [kimi-k3] eighth bit (bit 7) doesn't draw — it shifts the colour phase of that byte's seven pixels (the
2026-07-24 20:57:02 [kimi-k3] infamous "colour set" bit).
2026-07-24 20:57:02 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:57:02 [kimi-k3] • Addressing is weirder than text.* The rows interleave in groups of 8 with a 1 KB stride
2026-07-24 20:57:02 [kimi-k3] pattern. Row `y`'s address is:
2026-07-24 20:57:02 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:57:02 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:57:02 [kimi-k3] base + (y & 7) * 1024 + ((y >> 3) & 7) * 128 + (y >> 6) * 40 + (x >> 3...
2026-07-24 20:57:02 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:57:02 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:57:02 [kimi-k3] — honestly, nobody computes it by hand; everyone uses the ROM routine or a lookup table. The
2026-07-24 20:57:02 [kimi-k3] formula: `addr = 2000 + (y%8)400 + ((y/8)%8)80 + (y/64)*28 + x/7`. Worth verifying against
2026-07-24 20:57:02 [kimi-k3] the Red Book when you implement it; it's the classic source of off-by-one-row bugs.
2026-07-24 20:57:02 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:57:02 [kimi-k3] • Colour is a side effect, not data.* This is the part everyone trips on. The Apple II's
2026-07-24 20:57:02 [kimi-k3] hires colours emerge from NTSC artifacting: pixels in even vs odd columns produce different
2026-07-24 20:57:02 [kimi-k3] colours, and bit 7 flips which set:
2026-07-24 20:57:02 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:57:02 [kimi-k3] - bit 7 clear: even columns ≈ purple, odd columns ≈ green
2026-07-24 20:57:02 [kimi-k3] - bit 7 set: even columns ≈ blue, odd columns ≈ orange
2026-07-24 20:57:02 [kimi-k3] - adjacent 11 pixel pairs: white; adjacent 00: black
2026-07-24 20:57:02 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:57:02 [kimi-k3] So six colours, but only via a 2-pixel effective resolution for colour, and with fringing
2026-07-24 20:57:02 [kimi-k3] artifacts. Your `▀`-block terminal trick doesn't map onto this at all — you'd be rendering at the
2026-07-24 20:57:02 [kimi-k3] pixel level (280×192), e.g. with two vertical pixels per `▀` cell at 140×96 half-block cells, or
2026-07-24 20:57:02 [kimi-k3] doubling horizontally.
2026-07-24 20:57:02 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:57:02 [kimi-k3] • The switches.* `C057` (hires on), `C056` (lores on), plus the same `C050/51`
2026-07-24 20:57:02 [kimi-k3] graphics/text and `C052/53` full/mixed, and `$C054/55` page. Mixed hires keeps the 4-line text
2026-07-24 20:57:02 [kimi-k3] window over the bottom 32 pixel rows.
2026-07-24 20:57:02 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:57:02 [kimi-k3] • Now, the good news: Integer BASIC has no hires commands.* `HGR`, `HPLOT`, `HCOLOR` are
2026-07-24 20:57:02 [kimi-k3] Applesoft additions. On Integer BASIC, hires was reached only via `POKE`ing the soft switches and
2026-07-24 20:57:02 [kimi-k3] the bitmap directly (or `CALL`ing ROM routines). So for Brick Out and the language on your markdown
2026-07-24 20:57:02 [kimi-k3] list, hires is out of scope. The soft-switch handling you're already building (`$C057` case) can
2026-07-24 20:57:02 [kimi-k3] just set a flag for now, and hires rendering can be a separate milestone when you care about the
2026-07-24 20:57:02 [kimi-k3] Applesoft/hires-demo catalogue.
2026-07-24 20:57:02 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 20:57:02 [kimi-k3] If you do implement it later, the honest recommendation: render it as monochrome first (280×192,
2026-07-24 20:57:02 [kimi-k3] white-on-black), get the addressing right, and add artifact colour afterwards — many Apple II
2026-07-24 20:57:02 [kimi-k3] programs were written for and look correct on the monochrome interpretation.
2026-07-24 20:57:02 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 21:01:07 [simon@midnightblue.local] ok, in future i could use " ▘▝▀▖▌▞▛▗▚▐▜▄▙▟█" quads and 140x96 terminal but monochrome
2026-07-24 21:01:28 [simon@midnightblue.local] will be a bit useless no? even mono chrome monitors would display patterns for the colors
2026-07-24 21:02:02 [kimi-k3] 🟠nitrologic Relay 1.9.9 kimi-k3 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.483 530.8KB 34.00s
2026-07-24 21:02:02 [kimi-k3] You're right on both counts, and that quad-character string is already sitting in your file as
2026-07-24 21:02:02 [kimi-k3] `gridQuads` — so the idea fits your codebase nicely. Two vertical pixels per cell is what `▀` gives
2026-07-24 21:02:02 [kimi-k3] you; going to quads (`▘▝▖▗` etc., 2×2 pixels per cell) gets you 140 × 96 cells, which is
2026-07-24 21:02:02 [kimi-k3] exactly half-resolution hires and maps 2×2 pixel blocks perfectly onto one glyph. That's the
2026-07-24 21:02:02 [kimi-k3] natural terminal encoding for hires.
2026-07-24 21:02:02 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 21:02:02 [kimi-k3] And you're correct about monochrome being misleading. Even the period "green screen" monitors
2026-07-24 21:02:02 [kimi-k3] weren't showing raw bits for colour content — the NTSC artifact colours became distinct
2026-07-24 21:02:02 [kimi-k3] luminance/dot patterns (purple vs green have different densities and phases of on-pixels), so
2026-07-24 21:02:02 [kimi-k3] software that relied on colour was still legible, just texture-y. A flat white-on-black
2026-07-24 21:02:02 [kimi-k3] monochrome render would actually be less faithful than the patterned reality — dithered
2026-07-24 21:02:02 [kimi-k3] regions would flicker between solid white and half-density depending on phase.
2026-07-24 21:02:02 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 21:02:02 [kimi-k3] Practical options when you get there, roughly in effort order:
2026-07-24 21:02:02 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 21:02:02 [kimi-k3] 1. 6-colour simulation at 140×96 quads — decode each pair of adjacent pixels + column
2026-07-24 21:02:02 [kimi-k3] parity + bit-7 phase into the 6 hires colours, treat each 2×2 block's colours as the cell's pixels.
2026-07-24 21:02:02 [kimi-k3] Colours within a quad cell can differ per sub-pixel, but the quad glyph is monochrome, so you'd
2026-07-24 21:02:02 [kimi-k3] need fg/bg per cell — meaning quantising 4 sub-pixels to 2 colours. Lossy but readable.
2026-07-24 21:02:02 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 21:02:02 [kimi-k3] 2. 140×96 with `▀` and true fg/bg — 1×2 sub-pixels per cell, each cell shows exactly 2
2026-07-24 21:02:02 [kimi-k3] horizontally-adjacent pixels with two colours. This is the clean mapping: each `▀` = one
2026-07-24 21:02:02 [kimi-k3] even/odd pixel pair, which is precisely the unit of hires colour. No quantisation loss at all. 140
2026-07-24 21:02:02 [kimi-k3] columns is wide for a terminal but workable.
2026-07-24 21:02:02 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 21:02:02 [kimi-k3] 3. Monochrome with phase-aware dithering — render on/off pixels faithfully per the colour
2026-07-24 21:02:02 [kimi-k3] artifact patterns. Most faithful to a period green-screen, but as you say, "useless" for actually
2026-07-24 21:02:02 [kimi-k3] seeing which colour was intended.
2026-07-24 21:02:02 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 21:02:02 [kimi-k3] Option 2 is the sweet spot when the time comes: it's the same `▀` machinery you've already built
2026-07-24 21:02:02 [kimi-k3] for lores, just driven from `$2000` with a different address decoder and a 6-entry palette indexed
2026-07-24 21:02:02 [kimi-k3] by (parity, phase, pixel pair).
2026-07-24 21:02:02 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 21:05:39 [simon@midnightblue.local] in deno with console in raw mode can i still support system clipboard
2026-07-24 21:06:07 [kimi-k3] 🟠nitrologic Relay 1.9.9 kimi-k3 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.484 533.2KB 27.83s
2026-07-24 21:06:07 [kimi-k3] Short answer: reading yes, writing no — with caveats on the reading.
2026-07-24 21:06:07 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 21:06:07 [kimi-k3] Deno has `navigator.clipboard`, gated behind the `--allow-clipboard-read` /
2026-07-24 21:06:07 [kimi-k3] `--allow-clipboard-write` permission flags (or `--allow-clipboard` for both). Raw stdin mode
2026-07-24 21:06:07 [kimi-k3] doesn't interfere with the API itself — it's a Deno runtime feature, independent of TTY state.
2026-07-24 21:06:07 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 21:06:07 [kimi-k3] The catch is in how the two halves behave:
2026-07-24 21:06:07 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 21:06:07 [kimi-k3] • `navigator.clipboard.readText()` — works, but it's how you poll the clipboard.*
2026-07-24 21:06:07 [kimi-k3] On macOS/Linux this reads the system clipboard content as a string. So if your goal is "user copies
2026-07-24 21:06:07 [kimi-k3] something in another app, then pastes into my Apple II" (e.g. typing a BASIC listing in via
2026-07-24 21:06:07 [kimi-k3] clipboard rather than keystrokes), you can read it on demand — say, bound to a hotkey in your
2026-07-24 21:06:07 [kimi-k3] `onInput` handler — and then feed it through `pushKey` character by character. That path is fully
2026-07-24 21:06:07 [kimi-k3] workable today and is actually a really nice fit for your project: pasting period magazine listings
2026-07-24 21:06:07 [kimi-k3] straight into Integer BASIC.
2026-07-24 21:06:07 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 21:06:07 [kimi-k3] • `navigator.clipboard.writeText()` — also works*, and raw mode doesn't block it either. So
2026-07-24 21:06:07 [kimi-k3] "copy the Apple II screen contents to the system clipboard" (e.g. `LIST` output capture) is
2026-07-24 21:06:07 [kimi-k3] feasible too.
2026-07-24 21:06:07 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 21:06:07 [kimi-k3] • What raw mode does take away: the terminal's own selection/clipboard
2026-07-24 21:06:07 [kimi-k3] integration is unaffected (that's the terminal emulator's feature, not yours — selecting text in
2026-07-24 21:06:07 [kimi-k3] iTerm2 with the mouse still works since your app never sees it). What you can't* rely on is
2026-07-24 21:06:07 [kimi-k3] Ctrl+C/Ctrl+V reaching you as "copy/paste" — in raw mode those arrive as bytes (`0x03`, `0x16`),
2026-07-24 21:06:07 [kimi-k3] and it's up to you to interpret them. So the wiring is: catch your chosen hotkey byte in `onInput`,
2026-07-24 21:06:07 [kimi-k3] call the clipboard API, route the result into the emulated keyboard.
2026-07-24 21:06:07 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 21:06:07 [kimi-k3] One gotcha: the Apple II keyboard expects uppercase with the high bit set in your `pushKey` model —
2026-07-24 21:06:07 [kimi-k3] a pasted lowercase listing will need uppercasing (and Integer BASIC only accepts uppercase keywords
2026-07-24 21:06:07 [kimi-k3] anyway, so that's historically appropriate).
2026-07-24 21:06:07 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] 🟠nitrologic Relay 1.9.9 kimi-k3 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.563 625.9KB 38.44s
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] The clipboard work has begun — `pasteMachine` exists and there's a placeholder `"PASTEKEYHERE"`
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] case in the F-key handler. A few problems to flag before it bites:
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] • 1. `pasteMachine` iterates the string wrong.*
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] for (const keyCode of text) {
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] 	game.pushKey(keyCode);
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] }
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] `for...of` over a string yields characters (strings), not numbers, but `pushKey` expects a
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] `number`. TypeScript should complain (`string` not assignable to `number`) — unless it compiled
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] because `keyCode` is untyped somewhere. Either way you want:
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] for (const ch of text) {
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] 	game.pushKey(ch.toUpperCase().charCodeAt(0));
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] }
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] The `toUpperCase()` matters because Integer BASIC rejects lowercase input, as discussed. Also
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] consider translating `\n` → `\r` (13): the Apple II expects carriage return, and clipboard text has
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] Unix newlines.
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] • 2. `navigator.clipboard.readText()` is async.* It returns a `Promise<string>`, and you're
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] passing the promise straight into `pasteMachine`. The handler needs to be async:
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] const paste = await navigator.clipboard.readText();
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] which means the enclosing arrow/switch context needs `await` support — `onInput` would become
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] `async`, and the loop calling it should `await onInput(bytes)`. Alternatively fire-and-forget with
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] `.then()`. Without awaiting, you'll push the string `"[object Promise]"` character by character —
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] which will at least be entertaining the first time.
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] • 3. `case "PASTEKEYHERE":` is unreachable and a type error.* The surrounding `switch`
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] matches on `bytes.subarray(...).join(",")` — a string of comma-separated numbers like
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] `"27,91,50,52,126"`. A case label of `"PASTEKEYHERE"` never matches. You need to pick an actual
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] F-key sequence and put its numeric form there. F11 in many terminals sends `ESC [ 2 3 ~` →
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] `"27,91,50,51,126"`. So:
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] case "27,91,50,51,126": { ... paste logic ... }
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] • 4. Deno permissions.* You'll need `--allow-clipboard-read` on the command line (and
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] `--allow-clipboard-write` later if you add screen-copy). Without it the promise rejects — so wrap
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] in try/catch and log, or the unhandled rejection takes the machine down mid-paste.
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] • 5. Pace the paste.* Your `keyBuffer` mechanism drains as the ROM polls `$C000`, which
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] should naturally throttle — good design. But a 500-line paste will take a while at keyboard polling
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] speed; that's authentic and fine, just expect it.
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] Also still outstanding from the last review: the `splitY` transposition (`[0,0,24,20]` — LORES and
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] MIXED are swapped), and soft-switch writes still halt in `writeAddress`.
2026-07-24 21:16:07 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 21:19:40 [simon@midnightblue.local] do deno does not support clipboard?
2026-07-24 21:20:07 [kimi-k3] 🟠nitrologic Relay 1.9.9 kimi-k3 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.567 628.7KB 26.50s
2026-07-24 21:20:07 [kimi-k3] Deno does support clipboard — `navigator.clipboard.readText()` and `writeText()` are real,
2026-07-24 21:20:07 [kimi-k3] available by default in recent Deno versions, gated behind `--allow-clipboard-read` /
2026-07-24 21:20:07 [kimi-k3] `--allow-clipboard-write` permission flags.
2026-07-24 21:20:07 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 21:20:07 [kimi-k3] My point 4 wasn't "no support", it was: if you run without the flag, the promise rejects
2026-07-24 21:20:07 [kimi-k3] with a permission error at call time. Deno may also interactively prompt for permission if stdin is
2026-07-24 21:20:07 [kimi-k3] a TTY — but your stdin is in raw mode being pumped by `pollInput`, so the prompt could behave
2026-07-24 21:20:07 [kimi-k3] oddly. Pass the flag explicitly and it's a non-issue:
2026-07-24 21:20:07 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 21:20:07 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 21:20:07 [kimi-k3] deno run --allow-clipboard-read machine.ts
2026-07-24 21:20:07 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 21:20:07 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 21:20:07 [kimi-k3] (plus whatever flags you already use — `--allow-read`/`--allow-write` for the WAV files, and
2026-07-24 21:20:07 [kimi-k3] `--unstable-*` flags if any of your `system.ts` APIs need them.)
2026-07-24 21:20:07 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 21:20:07 [kimi-k3] So the API is fine; the actual fixes needed in your code are points 1–3 from before: iterate
2026-07-24 21:20:07 [kimi-k3] characters with `charCodeAt`, `await` the promise, and replace `"PASTEKEYHERE"` with a real
2026-07-24 21:20:07 [kimi-k3] escape-sequence match.
2026-07-24 21:20:07 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 22:40:38 [simon@midnightblue.local] when not in raw mode i need to wait for return key for all keyboard input?
2026-07-24 22:41:12 [kimi-k3] 🟠nitrologic Relay 1.9.9 kimi-k3 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.520 630.0KB 32.56s
2026-07-24 22:41:12 [kimi-k3] Yes — in cooked (non-raw) mode, the terminal line-discipline buffers input and only
2026-07-24 22:41:12 [kimi-k3] delivers it to your program when Enter is pressed. That's the OS/TTY layer, not Deno: `Deno.stdin`
2026-07-24 22:41:12 [kimi-k3] reads in cooked mode get whole lines at a time, with line editing (backspace etc.) handled by the
2026-07-24 22:41:12 [kimi-k3] terminal itself.
2026-07-24 22:41:12 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 22:41:12 [kimi-k3] That's why your `runInput(false)` presumably puts the TTY into raw mode — without it:
2026-07-24 22:41:12 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 22:41:12 [kimi-k3] - single keys like your `D`/`R`/`S`/`T` debug commands wouldn't fire until you typed the key
2026-07-24 22:41:12 [kimi-k3] plus Enter;
2026-07-24 22:41:12 [kimi-k3] - arrow/F-key escape sequences would arrive all at once after Enter, batched into one read;
2026-07-24 22:41:12 [kimi-k3] - and Ctrl+C would kill the process (SIGINT) instead of arriving as byte `0x03`.
2026-07-24 22:41:12 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 22:41:12 [kimi-k3] So raw mode is the right choice for a machine emulator where keystrokes are events. The trade-offs
2026-07-24 22:41:12 [kimi-k3] you already have in hand:
2026-07-24 22:41:12 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 22:41:12 [kimi-k3] - You're responsible for echo (you don't echo — the emulated Apple II "echoes" via its screen
2026-07-24 22:41:12 [kimi-k3] render, which is the correct behaviour anyway);
2026-07-24 22:41:12 [kimi-k3] - Ctrl+C no longer kills the app — you handle quit yourself (plain ESC → `running=false`);
2026-07-24 22:41:12 [kimi-k3] - Pasting into the terminal delivers the paste as a byte stream you feed to `pushKey` — same as
2026-07-24 22:41:12 [kimi-k3] your planned clipboard path.
2026-07-24 22:41:12 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 22:41:12 [kimi-k3] One refinement worth knowing about: bracketed paste mode. If you enable it (`\x1b[?2004h`
2026-07-24 22:41:12 [kimi-k3] on startup, `\x1b[?2004l` on shutdown), terminals wrap genuine user pastes in markers (`\x1b[200~`
2026-07-24 22:41:12 [kimi-k3] … `\x1b[201~`). That lets you distinguish "user pasted 200 bytes" from "user typed fast", so you
2026-07-24 22:41:12 [kimi-k3] can route terminal pastes through `pasteMachine` automatically without a hotkey, and without the OS
2026-07-24 22:41:12 [kimi-k3] clipboard API at all. That's often the nicer solution for terminal apps — the user's normal Cmd+V
2026-07-24 22:41:12 [kimi-k3] just works. The Deno permission flag and the `readText()` path remain useful as a fallback for
2026-07-24 22:41:12 [kimi-k3] terminals that don't support bracketed paste.
2026-07-24 22:41:12 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 22:46:41 [simon@midnightblue.local] ctrl c causes sigint in cook mode, any other keys that cause instant behavior ?
2026-07-24 22:47:06 [kimi-k3] 🟠nitrologic Relay 1.9.9 kimi-k3 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.574 632.0KB 22.58s
2026-07-24 22:47:06 [kimi-k3] Yes — in cooked mode the TTY line discipline intercepts several control characters before your
2026-07-24 22:47:06 [kimi-k3] program ever sees them. The main set on POSIX systems (macOS included):
2026-07-24 22:47:06 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 22:47:06 [kimi-k3] • Signal-generating keys* (process-level, instant):
2026-07-24 22:47:06 [kimi-k3] - Ctrl+C → SIGINT (interrupt)
2026-07-24 22:47:06 [kimi-k3] - Ctrl+\\ → SIGQUIT (quit + core dump)
2026-07-24 22:47:06 [kimi-k3] - Ctrl+Z → SIGTSTP (suspend to background — your app freezes, shell shows `^Z`, `fg`
2026-07-24 22:47:06 [kimi-k3] resumes)
2026-07-24 22:47:06 [kimi-k3] - Ctrl+Y → SIGTSTP, but deferred until the process tries to read it (rarely
2026-07-24 22:47:06 [kimi-k3] enabled)
2026-07-24 22:47:06 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 22:47:06 [kimi-k3] • Line-editing keys* (consumed by the terminal, never reach you):
2026-07-24 22:47:06 [kimi-k3] - Backspace/Delete (VERASE) — erases a character from the line buffer
2026-07-24 22:47:06 [kimi-k3] - Ctrl+W (VWERASE) — erase previous word
2026-07-24 22:47:06 [kimi-k3] - Ctrl+U (VKILL) — erase the whole line
2026-07-24 22:47:06 [kimi-k3] - Ctrl+R (VREPRINT) — reprint the line
2026-07-24 22:47:06 [kimi-k3] - Ctrl+V (VLNEXT) — literal-next: makes the following control char literal
2026-07-24 22:47:06 [kimi-k3] - Ctrl+O (VDISCARD) — discard pending output
2026-07-24 22:47:06 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 22:47:06 [kimi-k3] • Flow control* (affects the pipe itself):
2026-07-24 22:47:06 [kimi-k3] - Ctrl+S (VSTOP) — pauses output. This is the nasty one: your emulator's
2026-07-24 22:47:06 [kimi-k3] `console.log` calls block, the machine appears frozen, and there's no on-screen hint why.
2026-07-24 22:47:06 [kimi-k3] - Ctrl+Q (VSTART) — resumes output.
2026-07-24 22:47:06 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 22:47:06 [kimi-k3] Ctrl+S/Q (XON/XOFF) deserve emphasis because they surprise people: an emulator that "randomly
2026-07-24 22:47:06 [kimi-k3] freezes" when someone hits Ctrl+S, and "unfreezes" mysteriously with Ctrl+Q.
2026-07-24 22:47:06 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 22:47:06 [kimi-k3] Two more worth knowing:
2026-07-24 22:47:06 [kimi-k3] - Ctrl+D (VEOF) — on an empty line, makes `read()` return 0 bytes (EOF), which your
2026-07-24 22:47:06 [kimi-k3] `pollInput` would likely surface as `null` → your code sets `running=false`. So Ctrl+D is a
2026-07-24 22:47:06 [kimi-k3] cooked-mode "quit" for you.
2026-07-24 22:47:06 [kimi-k3] - Ctrl+M / Enter — delivers the line (that's the whole buffering mechanism).
2026-07-24 22:47:06 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-24 22:47:06 [kimi-k3] All of these are configurable via `stty` (e.g. `stty intr ^T` moves SIGINT off Ctrl+C, `stty -ixon`
2026-07-24 22:47:06 [kimi-k3] disables Ctrl+S/Q flow control), but raw mode sidesteps the entire table at once — which is the
2026-07-24 22:47:06 [kimi-k3] standard practice for emulators, editors, and anything else wanting keystroke-level control.
2026-07-24 22:47:06 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-25 00:53:18 [simon@midnightblue.local] with appleii hires mode, how is memory used
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] 🟠nitrologic Relay 1.9.9 kimi-k3 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.051 513B 94.67s
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] Where it lives
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] Hi-res mode uses 8 KB per page, with two pages available:
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] - Page 1: `2000`–`3FFF`
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] - Page 2: `4000`–`5FFF`
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] You select the page and mode with soft switches in the `$C05x` range (page 1/2, graphics/text,
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] lo-res/hi-res, full/mixed screen). Two pages allow page-flipping for flicker-free animation.
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] Byte-to-pixel mapping
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] - Resolution is 280 × 192, but each byte holds only 7 pixels (bits 0–6), with 40 bytes per
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] scanline.
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] - Bit 7 doesn't display a pixel — it shifts the color phase. With the high bit clear you get the
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] violet/green pair; with it set, blue/orange. Whether a set bit shows as the first or second color
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] of the pair depends on whether it falls in an even or odd column. Two adjacent set bits blend into
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] white; clear bits are black. All of this is NTSC artifact color — on a monochrome monitor you just
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] get clean dots.
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] - 40 bytes × 192 rows = 7680 bytes displayed, leaving 512 bytes of "screen holes" (64 gaps
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] of 8 bytes each) that are invisible and often used as scratch space by programs and peripheral
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] cards.
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] The interleaved layout
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] Rows are not stored consecutively. Wozniak arranged addressing so the video circuitry's counters
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] could double as the DRAM refresh counters. The address of pixel row Y is:
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] `page_base + (Y mod 8)×400 + ((Y div 8) mod 8)×80 + (Y div 64)×$28 + X`
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] Practical consequences:
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] - The screen is split into three vertical bands of 64 rows each, and within a band the rows jump
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] around in `$400` steps.
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] - Plotting a single pixel requires a read-modify-write of the byte containing it (or XOR for fast
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] drawing).
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] - Most graphics routines use a precomputed table of 192 row base addresses rather than calculating
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] the formula each time.
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] Mixed mode
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] With the mixed-screen switch set, the bottom 4 lines show text pulled from text page 1
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] (`400`–`7FF`), while the top 160 lines show hi-res graphics — that's what gives you the familiar
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] command line under a drawing.
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] Interaction with BASIC
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] Applesoft programs load at `$801` and grow upward, so the pages eat into program space:
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] - `HGR` (page 1): your program and variables must fit below `$2000` (~6 KB usable)
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] - `HGR2` (page 2): everything must fit below `$4000`
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] This is why larger programs often need `HIMEM:` adjustments or poke the screen directly instead of
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] using Applesoft's shape tables.
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] Worth knowing
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] - On the IIe and later, double hi-res adds the auxiliary memory bank: another 8 KB shadow
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] page, giving 560 × 192 effective resolution and 16 colors, with bytes interleaved between main and
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] aux memory.
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] - The lo-res/text pages (`400`–`BFF`) use exactly the same interleaved addressing scheme in
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] miniature, which is why the two modes share so much circuitry.
2026-07-25 00:54:55 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-25 04:00:47 [simon@midnightblue.local] are the bits msb or lsb first?
2026-07-25 04:01:24 [kimi-k3] 🟠nitrologic Relay 1.9.9 kimi-k3 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.016 3.471KB 34.97s
2026-07-25 04:01:24 [kimi-k3] LSB first — bit 0 is the leftmost pixel of the byte's 7-pixel group, bit 6 the rightmost:
2026-07-25 04:01:24 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-25 04:01:24 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-25 04:01:24 [kimi-k3] bit:      7   6   5   4   3   2   1   0
2026-07-25 04:01:24 [kimi-k3] pixel:  (hue) 7th 6th 5th 4th 3rd 2nd 1st   ← left to right
2026-07-25 04:01:24 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-25 04:01:24 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-25 04:01:24 [kimi-k3] So a byte value of `01` lights the leftmost dot, `40` the rightmost, and `$80` alone shows nothing
2026-07-25 04:01:24 [kimi-k3] but shifts the palette for any set bits in that byte.
2026-07-25 04:01:24 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-25 04:01:24 [kimi-k3] This feels backwards if you're used to MSB-first formats (like most bitmap formats), but it matches
2026-07-25 04:01:24 [kimi-k3] the hardware: the video generator is a shift register clocking bits out in that order, which was
2026-07-25 04:01:24 [kimi-k3] the simplest design for Woz to implement.
2026-07-25 04:01:24 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-25 04:01:24 [kimi-k3] Practical consequence for plotting: for pixel X on a row, the byte is at column `X div 7` and you
2026-07-25 04:01:24 [kimi-k3] set bit `X mod 7` — then optionally set bit 7 if you need the shifted color pair. This is why
2026-07-25 04:01:24 [kimi-k3] Applesoft's `HPLOT` and most assembly plotting routines use a lookup table of bit masks (`01, 02,
2026-07-25 04:01:24 [kimi-k3] 04 ... 40`) indexed by `X mod 7`.
2026-07-25 04:01:24 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-27 18:41:57 [simon@midnightblue.local] position cursor in console with esc string
2026-07-27 18:42:45 [kimi-k3] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 kimi-k3 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.022 512B 47.64s
2026-07-27 18:42:46 [kimi-k3] The ANSI sequence for absolute cursor positioning is:
2026-07-27 18:42:46 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-27 18:42:46 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-27 18:42:46 [kimi-k3] ESC[{row};{col}H
2026-07-27 18:42:46 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-27 18:42:46 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-27 18:42:46 [kimi-k3] `ESC` is byte `0x1B`, written as `\x1b`, `\033`, or `\e` depending on the language. Rows and
2026-07-27 18:42:46 [kimi-k3] columns are 1-based, so `ESC[1;1H` is the top-left corner. The `f` terminator works the
2026-07-27 18:42:46 [kimi-k3] same as `H`.
2026-07-27 18:42:46 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-27 18:42:46 [kimi-k3] Relative movement and related sequences:
2026-07-27 18:42:46 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-27 18:42:46 [kimi-k3] ╭─────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────╮
2026-07-27 18:42:46 [kimi-k3] │  Sequence           │  Effect                                    │
2026-07-27 18:42:46 [kimi-k3] ├─────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────┤
2026-07-27 18:42:46 [kimi-k3] │  `ESC[{n}A`         │  up n rows                                 │
2026-07-27 18:42:46 [kimi-k3] │  `ESC[{n}B`         │  down n rows                               │
2026-07-27 18:42:46 [kimi-k3] │  `ESC[{n}C`         │  right n columns                           │
2026-07-27 18:42:46 [kimi-k3] │  `ESC[{n}D`         │  left n columns                            │
2026-07-27 18:42:46 [kimi-k3] │  `ESC[{n}G`         │  move to column n, same row                │
2026-07-27 18:42:46 [kimi-k3] │  `ESC[s` / `ESC[u`  │  save / restore cursor position            │
2026-07-27 18:42:46 [kimi-k3] │  `ESC[2J`           │  clear screen (often paired with `ESC[H`)  │
2026-07-27 18:42:46 [kimi-k3] ╰─────────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────╯
2026-07-27 18:42:46 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-27 18:42:46 [kimi-k3] Examples:
2026-07-27 18:42:46 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-27 18:42:46 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-27 18:42:46 [kimi-k3] print("\x1b[5;10HHello", end="")  # row 5, column 10
2026-07-27 18:42:46 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-27 18:42:46 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-27 18:42:46 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-27 18:42:46 [kimi-k3] printf '\e[5;10HHello'
2026-07-27 18:42:46 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-27 18:42:46 [kimi-k3] 
2026-07-27 18:42:46 [kimi-k3] One caveat on Windows: modern terminals (Windows 10+) support these, but virtual terminal
2026-07-27 18:42:46 [kimi-k3] processing may need enabling. In Python, calling `os.system("")` once at startup does it, or use
2026-07-27 18:42:46 [kimi-k3] the `colorama` package which handles it automatically.
2026-07-27 18:42:46 [kimi-k3] 
2026-08-01 03:27:00 [simon@midnightblue.local] how do keys get repored as hit on c64 hardware?
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 grok-build-0.1 𝕏 🪠 0.7° $0.002 617B 17.93s
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] The Commodore 64 does not have dedicated keyboard hardware that "reports" key presses like
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] a modern keyboard controller. Instead, it uses a simple 8×8 keyboard matrix that is
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] actively scanned by the CPU through CIA #1.
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] How the Hardware Works
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] The keyboard is wired as a matrix:
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] - 8 columns are controlled by Port A of CIA1 ($DC00)
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] - 8 rows are read from Port B of CIA1 ($DC01)
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] ╭────────────────┬─────────────┬────────────────────────╮
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] │  CIA Register  │  Direction  │  Purpose               │
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] ├────────────────┼─────────────┼────────────────────────┤
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] │  $DC00 (PRA)   │  Output     │  Column select         │
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] │  $DC01 (PRB)   │  Input      │  Row read              │
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] ╰────────────────┴─────────────┴────────────────────────╯
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] How a Key Press is Detected
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] To detect if any key is pressed, the computer does the following (this is done in the IRQ routine
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] ~60 times per second):
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] 1. Set up the ports (done once at startup):
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1]    - CIA1 DDRA = $FF (Port A = all outputs)
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1]    - CIA1 DDRB = $00 (Port B = all inputs)
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] 2. Scan the matrix by walking through each column:
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1]    - Write a value to $DC00 with one column bit pulled low (the rest high).
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1]    - Read $DC01.
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1]    - If the value is not $FF, then one or more keys in that column are pressed.
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] Example of column selection:
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] - Column 0: write `FE` to DC00
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] - Column 1: write `FD` to DC00
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] - Column 2: write `FB` to DC00
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] - etc.
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] When you read $DC01, each bit that is low (0) means a key at the intersection of the
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] selected column and that row is currently pressed.
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] How the C64 Turns This Into a "Key Hit"
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] The raw matrix scanning is just the first step. The KERNAL does several things with it:
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] 1. Debounce – It compares the current scan against the previous scan.
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] 2. Detect new presses – It looks for keys that went from not-pressed → pressed.
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] 3. Handle modifiers – Checks the status of SHIFT, CTRL, and C= keys.
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] 4. Translate – Uses lookup tables to convert the row/column position into a PETSCII code.
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] 5. Store result:
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1]    - `C5` = current key matrix position (or 40 if no key)
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1]    - `$C6` = number of characters in keyboard buffer
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1]    - `0277`–`0280` = keyboard buffer
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] Important Details
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] - The keyboard is polled, not interrupt-driven (except for the RESTORE key, which is wired
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] to NMI).
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] - Multiple keys can be detected at once in hardware, but the KERNAL normally only reports one at a
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] time (with some exceptions like SHIFT+key).
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] - Ghosting can occur if you press certain combinations of 3+ keys because it's a simple matrix
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] without diodes.
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] Quick Test in Assembly
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] If you wanted to see raw key detection yourself:
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1]     lda #$fe        ; select column 0
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1]     sta $dc00
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1]     lda $dc01       ; read rows
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1]     ; If bit 0 is clear, the key at column 0, row 0 is down (INST/DEL)
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] 
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] Would you like me to show how the actual KERNAL keyboard scan works (the part that detects
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] new key hits vs held keys), or how to read raw matrix state reliably in your own code?
2026-08-01 03:27:18 [grok-build-0.1] 